## 15. Additional original features that make the platform more connected

The following are new proposals built from the principles in the attachment. They should be prioritised by value and dependencies, not all built at once.

### 15.1 Field Evidence Timeline

#### UI/UX + state + data contract — Field Evidence Timeline

Place the timeline as a dedicated `History` subview in Farm/Field Detail and as an expandable section in Deep Analysis, not as a duplicate global feed. Desktop composition: primary chronological timeline/list occupies 7 of 12 columns; selected-event evidence/detail panel occupies 5 columns. On mobile use a single-column vertical timeline with a sticky date/filter control. Use a solid/strong surface (`--fs-glass-strong`, 86% opaque if over the hero image), 12px radius, 20px padding; row/event padding 12–16px; date labels 12–13px; event title 14–15px; evidence summary 13–14px. Show a thin vertical timeline rail and small 8px event nodes; line/icon shape distinguishes event categories in addition to colour. Avoid a large separate card for every event.

Each entry renders a concise verb/title, timestamp in field timezone, farm/field label, event/source type (`remote sensing`, `weather/reanalysis`, `farmer reported`, `calculated`, `plan change`, `alert`, `analysis`, `task`), status and linked entity. Filters are time window, event type and crop/field; selected dates propagate to the map/date series only if the target source has a compatible interval. The event click opens its source report, task or journal entry with exact IDs. A “view as known then” action, if implemented, must reconstruct the snapshot available at that time—not display today's source values beneath an old event.

Use an append-only `EvidenceTimelineEvent` projection from canonical records; do not invent separate shadow records for the same task/report. Sort by event time then stable ID; retain both `observedAt`/`effectiveAt` and `recordedAt` where late-entry events are possible. Never infer an event date from retrieval time. API/query is scoped by farmId + fieldId + range + category and paginated. Cache key includes those filters. Empty, partial and source-unavailable states explain that historical records may be incomplete. Tests prove a completed task, new report, edited geometry and farmer observation each appear once with correct timestamp/context.


A unified timeline combining observations, farmer-entered events, alerts, analyses, plan changes and completed actions for one field. Use event type and source labels; filter by season or data category. It answers “what happened to this field, and what did Field Shift know at the time?” This connects satellite history, Deep Analysis, irrigation records, alerts and crop changes.

### 15.2 Data Gap Coach

#### UI/UX + prioritisation + calculation contract — Data Gap Coach

Render this as a contextual callout adjacent to the metric/assessment affected by missing evidence, and a compact prioritized checklist in the Data & Trust Centre. It must not become a giant generic card occupying the Home hero. Use `--fs-glass-default` (76% opaque) for a small image-backed callout, 12px radius and 16px padding. The hierarchy is: `What is missing` (15–16px semibold), `Why it matters` (13–14px), `Suggested next step` (13–14px), and one CTA. Use a neutral info icon with amber only when it denotes reduced evidence coverage; do not style missing data as a crop-health failure.

Prioritise gaps by decision relevance, expected usefulness, whether the farmer can realistically provide the data, and which current result depends on it. Example actions: confirm soil texture, enter pH from a named/date-stamped test, record crop history, confirm irrigation routine or wait for the next usable satellite composite. Each suggestion names the affected metric/decision and shows the dependency chain. Do not promise “confidence +12%” unless the confidence model estimates and validates that exact delta; otherwise say qualitatively what the input improves.

Model each recommendation as `DataGapRecommendation` with `gapKey`, `entityId`, `affectedMetricKeys[]`, severity/priority rationale, required input schema, evidence gap and route target. Dismissing one item suppresses only that gap version; if the dependency changes or the missing input becomes available, recompute the checklist. Clicking CTA opens the correct form with the missing field focused and returns to the prior page when saved/cancelled. Data-gap calculations are derived from the metric registry and analysis input snapshot, not a static checklist. Tests confirm that entering data resolves only its relevant gaps and does not automatically assert that the result became correct.


Instead of only showing that confidence is low, make it actionable. Identify which missing input is most likely to improve a specific decision and offer the next step: confirm soil texture, enter a measured pH with test date, add previous crop history, or verify irrigation quantity. Estimate the confidence benefit only if the confidence model has been validated; otherwise state qualitatively why the information matters.

### 15.3 Input Freshness and Change Impact

#### UI/UX + dependency invalidation contract — Input Freshness and Change Impact

Use an inline `Out of date` chip beside each affected metric/plan/report and a small impact drawer opened from `Why is this out of date?`. Chip geometry: 12–13px text, 6px vertical/9px horizontal padding, 8px radius; neutral amber/surface background rather than neon border. The drawer is 420–520px on desktop, full-height on mobile, with 96% opaque surface, 20px radius and 24px padding. It lists `Changed input`, `Affected outputs`, `Why`, `Last calculated`, and `Recalculate` action. Do not cover the data value with an unexplained red banner.

Build a dependency graph from `metricKey → input/source/geometry/profile/model versions → derived outputs`. Mutating a boundary invalidates area/centroid and geometry-sampled aggregates; changing irrigation affects forward water balance and rotation scores; new crop history affects sequencing constraints; changing a display unit does not invalidate calculations. Invalidation is scoped to the affected farm/field/input version. Old reports remain immutable and marked historical; a new calculation produces a new version.

Implementation uses typed domain events such as `FieldGeometryVersionChanged`, `IrrigationAvailabilityChanged`, `CropHistoryUpdated`, `SourceObservationArrived`, `CropKnowledgeVersionChanged`. A central dependency service returns affected metric/plan/report IDs. Do not rely on arbitrary page-local boolean flags. Background source refresh shows `New data available` until the user or scheduled policy runs the appropriate recomputation. Tests ensure unrelated fields remain fresh, stale results never appear as current, and recompute refreshes dependent outputs while preserving old reports.


When a user edits a field's boundary, crop history, soil test, irrigation limit or crop plan, show which outputs may now be stale: rotation ranking, water balance, next actions, scheduled Deep Analysis baseline and calendar. Let the user rerun only the impacted calculations where safe. This avoids both unnecessary full recomputation and stale results presented as current.

### 15.4 Decision Journal / Plan Change Log

#### UI/UX + audit event contract — Decision Journal / Plan Change Log

Display this in a `Plan history` tab and as a compact change strip on the rotation detail page. Desktop uses a chronological table/timeline with 60–72px row height; columns include date, action, prior plan/version, new plan/version, actor and optional reason. Use 14–15px action text and 12–13px metadata. Each row opens a detail drawer showing the old/new values as a diff: removed values left/muted, added values right/selected tint; never rely on color alone. For images, only use crop thumbnails that correspond to the actual old/new plan.

When the farmer chooses a plan, explicitly record `selected`, `rejected`, `deferred` or `replaced` as a decision event; capture an optional reason (water, cost, equipment, market, management preference, other). The selected plan version remains separate from the model's `recommendedCandidateId`. Do not silently make the highest-ranked rotation active after a rerun. Any application of changed inputs requires explicit review and creates a versioned profile/plan update.

Persist `PlanDecisionEvent` with actor, timestamp, farmId, fieldId, candidate ID, prior/new active version, reason and source report ID. This record is append-only; corrections create superseding events. Do not encode decisions as free text inside the mutable Farm row. Event history respects access controls and data deletion policy. Tests verify that a new run, selection, rejection, and changed plan have correct stable references and never rewrite historical score/ranking data.


When choosing a rotation, capture the selected plan/version, date and optional farmer reason. When rejecting the top recommendation, ask optionally whether the issue was cost, market, equipment, water, personal preference or unavailable crop. Use this context to interpret future decision support, but don't alter scientifically grounded crop constraints without an explicit profile update.

### 15.5 Crop Rotation Review Gate

#### UI/UX + triggering rule contract — Rotation Review Gate

Show a compact review state beside the active rotation: `Plan current`, `Review suggested`, or `Review required` only when defined rules support that severity. The badge uses label + icon + low-opacity surface; amber is not a decorative accent. Place the message near the plan title and in Home priority actions only when a trigger exists. Opening it reveals the trigger, date, changed dependency, why the original assumption matters, and actions `Review plan`, `Compare alternatives`, `Dismiss suggestion` where permissible. Keep the old plan visible and active until the farmer confirms a replacement; don't imply a suggestion is a forced invalidation unless a genuine hard constraint is violated.

Review triggers derive from registered dependencies and versioned thresholds: material irrigation/water availability change, new crop history, boundary change, newly available reliable data, plan review-by date or a known hard constraint failure. Define trigger hysteresis/cooldown to avoid repetitive notification churn and store `triggerRuleVersion`. State machine: `current → review_suggested → in_review → current` after explicit acceptance/revalidation, or `review_required` when a documented constraint no longer holds. Dismissal suppresses the same trigger fingerprint until relevant input changes, but does not erase the audit record.

The rule engine emits a `RotationReviewTrigger` containing the exact input/report IDs and reason. Test false positives, repeated runs with no change, a boundary edit, new crop history, and user choosing to retain the plan despite a noncritical suggestion.


Every active rotation has a review date and trigger conditions—such as new crop history, a major water-availability change, a boundary edit or evidence showing that a critical assumption no longer holds. The system flags “Plan review recommended,” not “plan invalid,” and explains the change. User-confirmed plans remain active until a user accepts a replacement, except where a clear safety limitation demands an alert.

### 15.6 Farm Resource Calendar

#### UI/UX + event projection contract — Farm Resource Calendar

This is implemented within Calendar & Actions rather than as a second disconnected calendar. Add filters for operation class (`planting/harvest`, `irrigation`, `soil test`, `scouting`, `fertilizer`, `rotation transition`, `analysis/review`, `farmer task`). On desktop use the calendar 8-column / agenda 4-column layout specified in Section 30.14. Add a compact workload-density strip above the month only when it helps identify clustered work. Each operation row displays field, proposed date/window, certainty basis (`planned`, `recommended window`, `scheduled`, `completed`) and task state.

Resource scheduling may show conflicts such as many fields' planting windows overlapping, but only if crop timing and field assignments support the inference. Do not create exact labor hours or machine capacity without user-supplied/validated capacity data. If equipment/labor information is available, expose it as optional farmer-entered constraints. Calendar tasks derive from plan stages and recommendations; the UI must not create duplicate tasks just because a recommendation is shown in more than one page.

Task actions use `TaskCommand` and update one canonical Task entity. Creation/editing records due window, timezone, linked plan/report/finding, priority and optional reminder. When a plan changes, dependent planned events update as new versions and actual completed events remain unchanged. Exported ICS events use stable UID and timezone; update/cancel versions when supported. Tests check cross-farm same-day events, calendar/task consistency, timezone/DST transitions and no duplicate action creation.


Bring together crop operations, irrigation routine reminders, soil testing, scouting and rotation transitions. This gives farmers a single action timeline. Each recommended operation links to its evidence and can be completed or deferred. Only create tasks from valid models/rules or clear user reminders; don't invent activities to make the calendar look populated.

### 15.7 Rotation Diversity and Workload Balance (multi-field)

#### UI/UX + optimisation contract — Multi-field Coordination

This is a later-phase planning workspace, not a control added to the basic single-field wizard. Desktop view has a field list/table on the left (4 columns) and season/operation timeline on the right (8 columns), with filters for crop family, season/year, water resource and workload windows. Each field remains an independent plan row with its own crop sequence, soil/water inputs, scores and confidence. A group summary can show peak number of concurrent planting/harvest windows only if event dates and field areas are valid.

The optimizer searches combinations of already feasible field-level candidates using explicit farm-level constraints: shared water cap if supplied, capacity limits if supplied, non-overlapping operation windows when requested, crop diversity targets if specified, and existing field-specific constraints. Do not sacrifice a critical field-level agronomic hard constraint merely to optimize farm workload. Display Pareto-style trade-offs or ranked feasible combinations with explanation of what improves and what worsens; label objective weights and assumptions. If labor/equipment capacity isn't provided, only show calendar overlap, not a claim that resources will be insufficient.

Data model links `FarmCoordinationPlan` to exact `RotationPlanVersion` IDs per field; outputs are proposals and never overwrite active plans automatically. Scenario-only coordination remains separate until user reviews a change diff and confirms. Tests include zero constraints, one field with no feasible candidates, shared resource cap violation, differing field input versions and same-input deterministic output.


For farms with several fields, a farm-level planner can coordinate field-specific rotations so planting and harvesting labour does not peak on every field at once, where the crop calendar and constraints permit. It should respect field-specific soil and water differences and show trade-offs rather than forcing all fields to share one rotation. This is a high-value future feature because the user manages multiple fields, but it depends on a reliable per-field rotation engine first.

### 15.8 Seasonal Farm Review

#### UI/UX + workflow contract — Seasonal Farm Review

Launch from a `Review season` action when a crop season reaches its configured review window; do not auto-assert a season ended based solely on a guessed calendar date. Use a stepper/wizard with progress, one decision at a time and a final review summary. Step groups: planted crop and dates; actual field events; recorded issues; completed/deferred recommendations; optional yield/cost/notes; final validation. Input panel uses 76% opaque glass over a quiet photo backdrop or solid surface, 24px padding and 12px radius. Show crop photo only where it helps identify a crop, not on every question. User can skip unknown values with explicit `Unknown` state; never fill a yield value to complete the form.

On completion, create a `SeasonReview` record that references planned RotationPlanVersion and CalendarEvent IDs; differences between planned and actual planting/harvest dates are stored as separate actual event records. Results may update future crop-history inputs only after the farmer confirms which review details should become canonical history. Old analyses remain unchanged and the next new analysis snapshots the confirmed additions.

The system can compare plan-vs-actual timing, recorded actions and user-reported observations. It cannot claim that a recommendation caused a yield change. If yield is absent, say no yield record was provided; do not use default estimates as if harvested. Tests verify skipped fields, late review submission, changed planned dates and correct carry-forward to a new rotation draft.


At the end of a season, guide the farmer through a brief review: what was planted, key events, any issues, recorded yields if they choose to supply them, and which recommendations were completed. Compare the planned calendar with actual dates and observations. Update the next planning cycle using confirmed history, not guessed yield values.

### 15.9 Recommendation Impact Review

#### UI/UX + evidence attribution contract — Recommendation Impact Review

Embed a compact `After this action` section in the relevant report and action detail, and provide a full comparison only when enough post-action data exists. Use a 6-column left/right comparison: action and completion record on the left; supported metric timeline/delta on the right. The summary says what happened after the recorded action, when the indicator changed and what other factors remain unaccounted for. Each delta is linked to the exact source window and metric method. Use `Observed after action` or `Associated change`; reserve causal wording for a separately validated causal design.

Eligibility requires: (1) a valid action record with completion date; (2) pre-action and post-action values from comparable metric definitions/time periods; (3) data coverage/quality above a declared minimum; and (4) the comparison window is suitable for the measured property. If these conditions fail, show `Not enough comparable evidence yet` with the reason. The UI must not treat a dismissed task as a completed action. A farmer-reported completion is labelled as reported, not sensor-verified.

`ImpactReview` stores action ID, baseline/current report/metric IDs, time windows, comparability flags, confounder caveats and engine version. It should be recalculated as a new immutable review if data changes, not mutate an earlier explanation. Tests cover no baseline, missing post values, incomparable methods and a real recorded action with valid measurements.


When enough relevant evidence exists, show whether a previously recommended action was followed and whether the associated indicator later changed. Use wording such as “After the action, this indicator increased,” not “the action caused improvement,” unless causal evidence exists. This creates accountability without overclaiming causality.

### 15.10 Farm readiness and data coverage scorecard

#### UI/UX + metric governance contract — Data Readiness Scorecard

Display as a checklist/progress indicator in farm setup, Data & Trust and optionally Farm Detail settings. Do not style it like a farm-health gauge. Use a neutral data-completeness treatment with a textual state such as `Profile completeness: 6 of 9 useful inputs`, plus grouped categories `Field geometry`, `Crop history`, `Soil evidence`, `Water practices`, `Data connections`. Each checklist row includes status, why it matters and one CTA to fix or verify the data. Use 14–15px row labels, 12–13px helper metadata and a 2px progress track; no huge radial chart unless the user benefits from it.

The readiness numerator/denominator are derived from a versioned list of eligible inputs for the requested analysis; optional fields must not be counted as required. Separate `not entered`, `unknown`, `stale`, `unavailable source` and `confirmed`. A user can legitimately say that a value is unknown. This is not a grade for the farmer and not an assessment of whether the land is healthy. The score must not directly become evidence confidence unless the confidence methodology defines a validated mapping.

Calculate readiness from `ReadinessRuleSetVersion` and the selected use case (e.g. rotation planning vs Deep Analysis). Selecting a row routes to the exact input and upon save updates only relevant checklist items. Test same data under different workflow requirements, optional unanswered questions, unavailable connectors and no false improvement from switching mode.


A non-judgemental checklist showing profile completeness: boundary saved, crop history, current plan, irrigation routine last confirmed, soil test present, data sources available. This is a **data readiness** measure, not a farm-health score. It helps the farmer understand how to improve analysis quality without confusing missing records with poor land conditions.

### 15.11 Exportable Field Brief

#### UI/UX + snapshot contract — Exportable Field Brief

The preview uses a document-style layout, not an app dashboard screenshot. Use the detailed page specification in Section 50 as authoritative: a restrained photographic cover occupying 20–28% of page height, clear title/scope/date, then findings, actions, crop plan and evidence notes. The in-app preview uses a 10–12-column document grid and a fixed preview toolbar with export/print actions; actual report contents are derived from an immutable report snapshot. Page size controls (A4/Letter) are presentation-only; they must not change metrics.

Export job state: `queued → generating → ready|failed`; show real job progress only if server reports it. If export is synchronous, use a truthful pending indicator without fake percentages. File content includes source date, method/version, units, limitations, selected farm/field context and demo label where applicable. The `Download` button is disabled until a valid file reference exists; failed generation exposes Retry. Do not claim a PDF has been created before the generation response is successful. Tests compare exported key metrics and report ID to on-screen report data.


Generate a structured report suitable for taking into the field: selected field map/boundary, current crop/stage, top observations, actions, due windows, water/soil caveats, source dates and a QR/link back to the report when available. A printable/low-bandwidth version supports real-world use. Never include unsupported diagnostics as facts.

### 15.12 Offline-tolerant field mode

#### UI/UX + local-sync state contract — Offline-Tolerant Mode

Show a compact connectivity badge in the global shell only when network state is genuinely detected; labels are `Online`, `Offline`, `Changes pending`, `Sync issue`. It must not be a decorative pulsing dot. Offline mode presents cached/available records with last-synced timestamps; use a subtle banner that explains which operations are unavailable. The local draft/editor remains usable for supported tasks, with an explicit queue count in the header and a sync-detail panel.

Use a local outbox pattern: each queued change has stable operation ID, entity ID, base version, payload schema version, created timestamp and conflict policy. Sync is idempotent and reports per-operation `pending`, `syncing`, `synced`, `conflict`, or `failed`. If the server has a newer entity version, do not blind-overwrite; show a conflict comparison. Do not queue unsupported jobs (like a true server-side scheduled run) as though they ran offline. Satellite imagery may be stale/cached, with a visible data-time label; no live map layer is implied when offline.

The local database must tolerate browser storage quota/error, and provide export/recovery guidance where practical. Background sync must run only if the browser/platform supports it; otherwise sync when the app becomes online/foregrounded. Tests cover offline create/update, duplicate sync replay, browser reload, local quota failure, and version conflict.


Later, cache the active plan, calendar, tasks and basic field records for intermittent connectivity. Queue farmer observations for sync when online, showing pending/synced/failed clearly. Keep high-cost, live satellite processing and cloud scheduled analysis separate from offline data entry.

### 15.13 What Changed / Evidence story

#### UI/UX + evidence-narrative contract — What Changed

This reusable module appears on Home as at most 2–3 concise change summaries and inside Deep Analysis as a full evidence-backed explanation. On Home, each summary is a compact row (icon/status, 14–15px headline, 13–14px supporting sentence, 12px timestamp/context); use a 1px divider rather than a bordered card per row. In the report, use a vertical narrative with each statement followed by a `View evidence` link and source-period chip. A selected finding opens the exact metric/chart point and Evidence Drawer.

Narrative generation is constrained to a set of typed facts/deltas produced by the comparison engine. Inputs include old/new metric values, baseline period, current period, changed source/profile/model metadata, comparable flag and driver categories. LLM phrasing may simplify wording but cannot invent a numeric cause or exceed the evidence payload. If a change falls inside the noise/uncertainty interval, use `No meaningful change can be confirmed`; if source methods changed, explain the methodology difference. Distinguish `input changed`, `new source data arrived`, `model version changed` and `metric value changed`.

Store the facts and references used for each generated summary so later investigation can reproduce it. Tests ensure every sentence containing a number maps to a registered metric and a source/derived value; no causal claim is generated from simple before/after correlation.


A reusable explanation module across Home and Deep Analysis that composes a short narrative only from supported deltas: “Rainfall observed over the last seven days was X versus the local baseline Y; the water-balance model consequently changed by Z. This is based on source A, updated at time B.” If no change is significant or data is not comparable, say that no meaningful conclusion can be made.

### 15.14 Avoid feature bloat

Do not build every idea in one release. The core product already has meaningful complexity. Prioritise the shared data model, truthful sources, two distinct planning/monitoring flows, useful reports and task integration. The advanced features above become valuable only when their inputs and underlying logic are reliable.

