### 27.2 Typography and hierarchy

Use one coherent modern sans-serif family (Inter, Geist or a similarly legible family). Use tabular numerals for values. Establish a visible scale; do not render every label and heading at the same size.

- Page title: typically 28–36px desktop, 24–30px mobile.
- Section title: 18–22px.
- Card heading: 15–18px, semibold.
- Body: 14–16px minimum for essential copy; prefer 15–16px.
- Chart labels/metadata: normally 12–13px, with zoom/help available; never use tiny labels for critical evidence.
- Hero metrics: responsive and large enough to read, but their labels and definitions must remain close.
- Helper text: concise and readable; no walls of tiny grey copy.

Use meaningful whitespace between page header, primary action, filters, content groups and footnotes. Use headings before cards. Avoid repeatedly nesting bordered cards inside bordered cards; use spacing, background tone and typographic hierarchy to group content.

### 27.3 Surface and card rules

- Rounded corners should be consistent and moderate, generally 8–14px, with larger radii reserved for hero panels or dialogs.
- No constant glow; no neon outline on every component; no floating unbounded pills; no excessive animated background.
- Use photographs only when they communicate crop/land context and are properly sized/cropped. Crop images need captions and accessible alt text where meaningful. Decorative imagery must not push key information below the fold.
- Use SVG/Lucide-style professional line icons, not emojis.
- Use a consistent icon size scale (16px inline, 18–20px controls, 22–24px section marks); do not use oversized icons to fill empty space.
- Data values, labels, units and provenance should form one readable cluster. Never detach a value's caveat two screens away.
- Use full-width tables when comparison is the point. Do not transform every table row into separate cards on desktop.
- A card should answer one clear question. Combine related weather facts in one coherent weather card rather than building a separate card for every number.

### 27.4 Grid, spacing and responsive layout

Use a responsive 12-column grid on wide desktop screens with consistent spacing. Main content should be close to full usable width, leaving balanced margins rather than an artificially narrow column. For wide tables and calendars, optimise for the viewport and only permit horizontal scrolling inside a clearly indicated data region when necessary—not on the whole page.

At each breakpoint verify: top navigation wrapping/collapse, table-to-list transformation, map tool placement, drawers converting to full-screen sheets on mobile, chart legibility, crop sequences reflowing vertically, sticky headers not covering content, and touch targets of at least 44×44 CSS pixels. Test desktop width 1440px, laptop 1280px, tablet 768px and mobile 390px/360px; these are representative test widths, not exclusive targets.

### 27.5 Shared components and their states

Build reusable components and specify the following states for every one:

- Button: default, hover, focus-visible, pressed, loading, disabled, success/error result.
- Input: empty, focused, filled, invalid, helper, read-only, saving, saved, save-failed.
- Select/combobox: closed, open, searching, no match, keyboard-selected, chosen, loading, failed.
- Card/list row: default, hover/clickable, selected, stale, loading, partial data, unavailable, actionable, resolved/archived.
- Tooltip/help popover: mouse hover and keyboard/focus/tap access; never require hover on mobile.
- Drawer/dialog: open, loading, content, validation error, save pending, save success, save failure, close with unsaved changes.
- Chart: loading, valid data, partial data, no data, failed source, selected point, accessible text/table alternative.
- Badge: icon + text + optional tooltip, with semantic label; no badge should be purely decorative.
- Empty state: explains why empty, what action creates content, and a real CTA.
- Toast: short outcome summary, optional Undo only when true undo is supported, auto-dismiss duration and accessible live-region announcement.

### 27.6 Motion rules

Use subtle 180–320ms interaction motion; wizard/page transitions may be around 250–450ms. Reversing a wizard step should move in the opposite direction. Use spring/layout motion only if it improves continuity. Motion must never delay a critical button or fake data progress. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`, stop ambient motion in hidden tabs and never rely on animation alone to convey a state change.

## 28. Global undo/redo, command search and in-app guidance

### 28.1 Undo/redo behaviour

Provide global Undo and Redo at the far-left of the top bar before the logo, as requested in the historical brief. These buttons operate on a central action history of reversible domain changes such as editing a draft answer, renaming a farm, moving a field polygon vertex, changing a planned date or modifying an unsaved scenario. The history label should say e.g. “Undo: move boundary point” or “Redo: change irrigation assumption.”

Rules:

- Save an action snapshot or reversible command before mutation.
- Group rapid repeated changes (e.g. drag vertices) into one meaningful history action, not hundreds of entries.
- Do not include passive events like receiving new weather data as user-undoable actions.
- Warn before undoing an action whose reversal may create a stale analysis.
- If undo reverts an input used by an analysis, restore the input and mark affected results stale; do not mutate the historical report.
- Undoing a map geometry edit restores the prior unsaved geometry; undoing a saved geometry is a new versioned correction, not deletion of history.
- History must not allow cross-user actions or cross-farm actions to be replayed after the context changes.
- Disable controls and explain when the selected operation cannot be safely undone.

### 28.2 Global “?” guide

The attachment requests a persistent `?` page guide. Include it consistently on major pages, wizard stages and farm details, but make it discreet. On activation, open a panel that explains: the current page's purpose; the visible sections and key numbers; what each action does; the recommended next 2–3 steps; and relevant glossary definitions. The guide content is contextual to the exact route and active mode, not a generic text dump. Allow closing, keyboard Escape, focus return and dismissible first-use tips. Do not let the guide block critical fields or cover save actions.

### 28.3 Global search / command launcher

A later but valuable enhancement: search farms, fields, crop plans, analysis reports, alerts, tasks and source/methodology entries by name. Include keyboard shortcut only if shown clearly and accessible. Results display type + parent context, so “Wheat” in Farm A cannot be confused with “Wheat” in Farm B. Selecting a result must route to the exact entity context. Search results must obey user permissions and should not index private report content into third-party services without consent.

## 29. Map workspace: complete interaction and geometry contract

### 29A. Quantified map materials, layer semantics and geometry editor states

**Compositional layout:** map workspace uses a `calc(100dvh - 64px)`-aware work area, but retains document scroll if supporting information extends below it. Desktop left navigator: 260–300px when expanded; central map canvas: flexible `minmax(0, 1fr)`; right inspector: 340–390px. At ≥1440px the map should remain at least about 62% of the available center workspace when both side panels are open; provide independent collapse controls. The left panel uses `--fs-glass-strong` (86% opaque) and 12px blur; right panel 90% opaque or solid because it carries small metadata; use 12px radius and 1px neutral border. The map imagery itself is not glass and never receives a whole-layer CSS hue/invert filter. Preserve tile attribution and provider usage rules.

**Map field-geometry symbology:** unsaved polygon = dashed 2px white/teal line plus small editable circular vertex handles; saved selected polygon = solid 2.5px teal outline plus low-opacity teal fill (`fillOpacity` about 0.12–0.18); saved unselected polygon = 1.5px white/slate outline and 0.05–0.08 fill; hover = 2px outline and a transient fill change; invalid geometry = high-contrast amber/coral dash plus an attached error label. These values must be tuned to preserve legibility against natural imagery. Do not apply blur to polygon edges. Vertex markers use a minimum 12px hit target even if the visible dot is 6–8px. Always show selected field identity in the inspector and map label/list.

**Map layer controls:** each row carries human label, layer type/source, date window and availability state. Toggle state controls the actual map renderer layer visibility, opacity or blend mode; the row cannot pretend data is displayed. Opacity slider controls raster opacity where renderer supports it and shows a percent label. Index legend includes units/range, palette endpoints, missing-data colour and selected date. Historical comparison mode synchronises both map panes by geometry version, projection, zoom/bounds and cursor state; toggling one pane's temporal frame must be explicit. If valid imagery is not available, the layer is disabled with a reason—not populated by synthetic colors.

**Geometry editor state machine:** `viewing → drawing → draft_valid|draft_invalid → editing_vertices → validating → save_pending → saved|save_failed`; user can `cancel` from draft/edit states with a discard-confirmation if geometry changed. Store polygon coordinates in GeoJSON with explicit CRS (WGS84 lon/lat at the API boundary); calculate area using an appropriate geodesic/projected method, store area units/method/version and do not naïvely use planar degree math. Validation includes polygon closure, at least 3 unique vertices, self-intersection, empty/near-zero area, coordinate ranges, duplicate vertices and plausible extents. Boundary save creates `GeometryVersion`; downstream map samples/areas are invalidated and previous reports keep their old geometryVersionId.

Use a documented Leaflet drawing/editing plugin (for example Leaflet-Geoman) or a justified equivalent; describe the exact library features used and license/plan constraints. Drawing controls require pointer, touch and keyboard alternatives where technically possible. Map failure states distinguish tile outage, geocoder failure, no georeference, invalid polygon, missing imagery and data-service failure. Research: Leaflet-Geoman documents polygon drawing/editing, snapping and geometry operations; MapLibre distinguishes raster sources from raster-style layers and exposes opacity/visibility as layer properties. Sources are cited in the research bibliography.


The map and drawing workflow is a core product capability, not an optional decoration. Provide it both during Add Farm/field setup and later within My Farms, with shared geometry logic so boundaries cannot diverge between workflows.

### 29.1 Map page layout

**Desktop:** map canvas fills the majority of the page (roughly 65–75% width); a contextual right-side inspector (roughly 25–35%) shows selected farm/field details, drawing instructions, layer controls and save status. A compact left list or top selector provides farm/field navigation. The exact proportions should adapt to viewport and inspector visibility. Never shrink the map into a small card surrounded by unused space.

**Top map toolbar:** search address/place, selected farm/field breadcrumb, map layers, basemap choice where available, fit-to-selection, locate-me, measurement/area units and Help. **Drawing toolbar:** Select, Draw field boundary, Edit vertices, Draw exclusion area/hole, Import boundary, Undo, Redo, Delete selected (with confirmation), Cancel edits and Save changes. Disable controls that do not make sense in the current mode and show their tooltip/reason.

**Right inspector sections:** field/farm identity; area/perimeter; location/centroid; boundary quality and unsaved changes; selected map layer and coverage note; field-specific crop/soil/water metadata; action links to “Open field overview,” “Plan crop rotations,” “Run Deep Analysis” and “View evidence.” The inspector must show source/measurement limitations for any overlay and never imply that a basemap is an agronomic sensor.

### 29.1A Visual direction for the map workspace — inspired by the supplied references, never copied

The two map-interface screenshots supplied by the user are **visual-quality references for the Maps & Fields experience only**. They communicate the level of professional geospatial information design desired: real satellite/aerial imagery as the dominant canvas; clearly defined farm/field geometry; dark, quiet analytical panels; meaningful map layers; historical imagery; and charts/metrics tied to the selected place and date. Use those principles to design an original Field Shift experience. Do not copy either product's branding, names, exact arrangement, wording, icon set, proprietary graphics, or pixel-level composition. Do not make the entire Field Shift product look like a clone of a mapping vendor. Build around Field Shift's own tasks: field management, crop-rotation decisions, water/soil understanding, evidence inspection and Deep Analysis.

#### Art direction and map canvas
- The map itself is the hero and should occupy most of the available workspace. On a wide desktop, target a usable map canvas of roughly 60–70% of the width remaining after global navigation and open side panels. The map must never be reduced to a small decorative card.
- Prefer true-colour, high-resolution satellite/aerial basemap imagery where a permitted, reliable provider is available. Retain the imagery's natural colours. **Do not apply a global dark/invert/hue filter to scientific imagery or index rasters** to force them into the app theme; this can distort the evidence. Make the surrounding UI dark, not the data itself.
- Place the dark Field Shift UI around the map: deep charcoal/navy surfaces, crisp thin neutral borders, controlled hierarchy, clear labels, small professional line icons and restrained teal/emerald accents. Use cyan/teal for selected items and interactive focus, amber for caution, red/coral for critical states, and muted slate for secondary information. Never communicate severity by colour alone.
- Use only restrained surface layering, shadows and occasional translucency. No neon edge glow, glowing map polygons, glass-effect overload, oversized icons, cartoon farmland or decorative 3D. The map's real imagery and the field geometry provide the visual richness.
- Draw field boundaries with a crisp 1.5–2 px high-contrast outline and a low-opacity, theme-aware fill. Selected field: stronger solid teal/white outline plus a clear field label; unselected fields: thinner muted outlines; hovered field: temporary highlight; actively edited boundary: distinct dashed/draft style and visible vertices. Ensure outlines remain visible over pale sand, dark soil, vegetation and cloud shadows. Provide a non-colour distinction, such as line style or label, where overlapping shapes would otherwise be ambiguous.
- Field names should sit on or next to their actual polygon when space permits, with collision avoidance. If labels would clutter the image, use numbered markers with a matching field list. Labels must move with the map and point to the correct geometry. Do not use sample names or fake polygons in a real user's map.

#### Original Field Shift layout: one map, contextual panels

Use the following as a layout system rather than copying the reference screenshot's fixed composition. Keep the global Field Shift navigation consistent with the rest of the app.

1. **Page header / map command bar:** immediately above the canvas, place a breadcrumb such as `Maps & Fields / [Farm] / [Field]`, a farm selector, field selector where applicable, search place/address, current data period when relevant, and compact primary actions. The primary action changes according to context: `Draw boundary` when no boundary exists; `Edit boundary` when a saved one is selected; `Compare dates` when valid historical observations exist. Secondary controls include `Fit field`, `Fit all fields`, `Locate me`, `Measure`, and help. Every icon-only control has an accessible name and tooltip.
2. **Farm & field navigator (collapsible left panel or drawer):** a searchable farm/field tree with hierarchy, field area, current crop if known, and concise status. Selecting a field highlights and fits that exact polygon, updates the entire inspector and records the selection in navigation context. Avoid showing multiple farms' numbers together without explicit per-farm labels. Collapse this panel to give the map more room; reopening it must not reset map extent or selected field.
3. **Dominant central map canvas:** show the selected farm/field geometry, chosen imagery/overlay, map controls and a compact legend. Use a floating, vertically arranged zoom control and a clear `Fit selection` control. Support pan, zoom, map identification (click an overlay/feature to inspect it), drawing and vertex editing. Keep the selected map extent stable when a layer is toggled unless the user explicitly requests a fit/zoom action.
4. **Contextual right inspector:** a narrow but readable panel, approximately 320–380 px on wide desktop where space allows, with its own collapse/expand control. Use contextual tabs such as `Field overview`, `Layers`, `History`, and `Compare`—not four separate permanent stacks. The inspector must update to the same selected farm, field, layer and observation date as the central map. It contains data controls and field evidence, not a second unrelated dashboard.
5. **Temporal strip / bottom timeline:** show it only when a selected data layer has a meaningful observation history. Display available observation dates, thumbnails only where genuine imagery exists, cloud/quality status where available, and the selected date. A compact date-range bar can be pinned to the lower edge of the map, but must not cover key labels or drawing vertices. For a single-date layer, do not invent a timeline.
6. **Layer legend and attribution:** position the legend near the lower edge of the map in a legible, compact panel, avoiding overlap with the timeline and drawing toolbar. Show the units, range and meaning of colours. Put map-tile attribution in the required visible location. Legend thresholds must come from the actual layer definition, not decorative arbitrary values.

#### Contextual workspace modes to keep information organized

Do not display every possible panel at once. The user's current intent chooses the panel arrangement:

- **Explore mode:** Farm & field navigator + satellite/basemap canvas + right `Layers` inspector. The inspector lists the currently available layers, their toggles, source, date, status and metadata drawer.
- **Field inspection mode:** reduce the navigator to a compact field selector; keep the chosen field dominant on the map; show the right `Field overview` with a concise subset of relevant status metrics, source labels and short historical sparkline only if supported data exists. Advanced users can expand technical values.
- **Historical comparison mode:** make the date selection obvious. Offer a synchronised before/after comparison using either side-by-side maps or a drag-to-reveal swipe slider, selected according to screen space. A narrow history/image strip may appear to the left of the map, while the right inspector shows the difference summary, chart and data table. Both views must use the same field boundary, projection, zoom and aligned extent. Clearly label each date and source.
- **Boundary edit mode:** reduce distracting telemetry and layer controls; prioritise vertices, geometry validation, live area, undo/redo and Save/Cancel. Preserve imagery as the working background. Show a persistent draft/unsaved status. Never make the user guess whether they are editing a saved boundary or a draft.
- **Evidence mode:** identify the exact raster, observation or metric being inspected. Show its date/range, legend, spatial resolution, valid-pixel/coverage information if available and source/processing notes. Keep farmer-entered data visibly distinct from remotely sensed, reanalysis, modelled or calculated data.

The system changes modes in place where practical, preserving farm, field, date and extent. If changing mode would discard unsaved geometry or other edits, ask the user to save, discard or stay.

#### Field overview panel: information design

The compact selected-field summary can contain the following, **only when each value has a defensible source or calculation**:
- Selected field name, parent farm, area, crop/rotation stage if recorded, and last updated time.
- Overall field condition/status from the defined assessment logic, with a plain-language explanation and a link to the latest Deep Analysis report. Never present an unexplained heart/health number as a measurement.
- A small set of useful indicators that depend on the field and chosen context—for example recent rainfall, crop-stage information, water balance, vegetation index or a validated soil metric. Each value uses a clear number, unit, short meaning, evidence date and state label (`Observed`, `Modelled`, `Farmer-reported`, `Estimated`, `Demo` or `Unavailable`). Do not fill a 2-column grid with metrics solely to imitate the reference.
- Compact trends or sparklines only if the underlying time series really exists, is date-aligned and has enough valid observations. Selecting a metric opens its full chart/history in the inspector and the correct detail page.
- `Open field overview`, `Open crop rotation`, `Run Deep Analysis`, `View history` and `View evidence` actions that navigate to the exact selected field and retain context. If the prerequisite plan/data is absent, the action must start the appropriate setup or explain the missing prerequisite rather than opening an empty view.

#### Layers & Data inspector

- Add a search/filter field only if the available layer catalog is large enough to justify it. Group layers into meaningful headings such as `Base map`, `Field boundaries`, `Vegetation`, `Weather & water`, `Soil & terrain`, and `Quality & comparison`.
- Each layer entry has a human-readable label, an on/off state where meaningful, an availability state, and a disclosure action to inspect source and definition. Distinguish a currently active layer from a pinned/favourite layer; a pin does not mean the source was fetched successfully.
- Candidate layers include true-colour imagery; field boundaries; actual NDVI/EVI/NDRE when a real, quality-screened satellite product is connected; rainfall or weather context; supported soil properties; elevation/slope; and quality/cloud masks. Do not promise every layer for every location/date. Layers such as nutrient or disease heatmaps require valid inputs and a documented model; do not present invented raster layers as observed data.
- When toggled on, show a real loading state, then either the data overlay and legend or an honest no-data/error explanation. Keep layer choices separate from boundary geometry. Switching off a layer never deletes data or geometry.
- If two overlays are incompatible, hide or disable the conflicting combination and explain why. Never combine multiple opaque rasters until the basemap and field edges become unreadable.
- Layer metadata must include, where applicable: source/provider, product/version, observation period, retrieval date, unit/index definition, nominal spatial resolution, processing method, valid-pixel/coverage state, quality caveats and any permitted download/source link.

#### Time-series and comparison visuals

- Selecting a date updates the map overlay, legend, statistics, table and weather/context values that are date-dependent. Use one canonical `selectedObservationPeriod` to prevent the map showing one date while the panel shows another.
- In comparison mode, provide `Before` and `After` date selectors and a visible difference layer only where arithmetic comparison is meaningful. Offer a swipe divider or synchronised side-by-side maps; never use an attractive before/after thumbnail that does not correspond to the actual selected data.
- For genuine raster values, useful statistics may include minimum/median/mean/maximum, distribution histogram, change distribution, valid area/pixel count and percentage of valid coverage. Define whether summary statistics are over the whole polygon or only valid pixels. If coverage is too low, suppress or qualify statistics rather than interpreting cloud/no-data pixels as poor crop condition.
- A chart beside the map should answer a clear question (e.g. “How did the vegetation index change across available observations?”). Title it with the selected field, metric, units and period. Put a one-sentence takeaway above it and an exact data table/source details behind a disclosure. Never show an empty chart with invented axes/data to fill space.
- The visual legend should be continuous or categorical according to the actual index's scientific meaning. State index units/range. Do not apply the same colours or thresholds to NDVI, EVI, NDRE, soil moisture and risk outputs as if they were interchangeable.

#### Interaction rules for the map visual reference

| User action | Required response |
|---|---|
| Select a field name in the left navigator | Highlight its outline, fit it on the map, update the right panel and make all related actions use that field ID. |
| Click a field polygon on the map | Select the same field in the navigator, show its label and open the contextual inspector. Overlap selection presents a small disambiguation list. |
| Toggle a layer | Fetch/render that layer if implemented; update legend, provenance and selected date without changing field identity or geometry. Show unavailable/failure states honestly. |
| Select an observation date | Update the map raster, date badge, chart, statistics and table to the same timestamp/window. |
| Choose Compare | Require two valid comparable periods, align both maps, show a before/after or difference view, and disclose incompatible data versions or insufficient coverage. |
| Click a chart segment/bin where supported | Highlight corresponding values or period on the map/timeline; otherwise do not pretend a non-linked chart is interactive. |
| Begin boundary drawing | Enter a visible edit mode, display vertices and live geometry, and temporarily de-emphasise irrelevant metrics. |
| Save a boundary | Validate and commit geometry, area/centroid and revision together, then show a success state and a list of calculations marked stale. |
| Open Deep Analysis from the map | Open it with the selected field preselected and the current boundary/data context recorded. |
| Collapse/expand a panel | Resize/invalidate the map canvas, keep the active geometry/layer/date, and prevent a grey or blank map. |

#### Responsive map layout

- **Wide desktop (1440 px and above):** use the three-region workspace only when both side panels are open; the map must retain enough width for meaningful field outlines. Start with one side panel collapsed if the available map canvas becomes cramped.
- **Laptop (1280 px):** keep the map central; make the field navigator collapsible and right inspector approximately 320 px or a drawer. Avoid horizontal page scrolling.
- **Tablet (768 px):** map remains the primary surface; field list and inspector become drawers or tabs. Put drawing controls into a compact toolbar with text labels available on expansion.
- **Phone (390/360 px):** map uses almost the entire main viewport. Keep only essential floating controls visible; use bottom sheets for field list, layer selection, details and date timeline. Drawing must support touch, vertex selection and a non-map alternative for manual coordinates. Do not attempt to squeeze the desktop three-column layout onto a phone.
- In every layout, ensure that the map does not steal page scroll unexpectedly, all controls remain tappable, panels do not cover the selected polygon without a way to reposition them, and reduced-motion preferences are respected.

#### Originality and quality bar

The reference images are a benchmark for density, clarity and professional map-centric composition—not a request for cloning. Field Shift must have its own component system, wording, iconography, labels, data hierarchy and colour tokens. Its strongest differentiator is the connection between the map and the crop-rotation plan, Deep Analysis, field history and recommendations. A user should be able to move from a detected field issue on the map to the evidence, the relevant assessment, and a practical next action without losing their selected field. Make that connected workflow the defining visual and functional feature.

### 29.2 Initial map loading and world bounds

- Render a single world; do not repeat the world horizontally. For Leaflet use a no-wrap tile configuration, world bounds, no world-copy jumping and a suitable minimum zoom. For MapLibre, disable world copies and set valid geographic bounds. Respect the chosen library's current official docs.
- Initial view fits the whole world without unnecessary clipping, then smoothly zooms to a supplied saved farm/field if one exists. If a saved boundary exists, fit the geometry with comfortable padding and preserve a sensible zoom cap.
- On resize, drawer open/close, or entering a hidden tab, call the map library's resize/invalidate-size operation; prevent blank or grey maps.
- Validate longitude in [-180, 180] and latitude in [-90, 90]; clamp search/fly-to requests to valid coordinates and explain invalid manual values.
- Provide zoom buttons and trackpad/mouse-wheel navigation; support pinch zoom on touch. Keyboard users need accessible map controls and a non-map list/form alternative.
- Map attribution must remain visible. Do not promise a public tile provider has 100% uptime; respect its usage policy and provide a graceful failure state.

### 29.3 Ways to add a farm or field

Support these routes where data/service capability allows:

1. **Search by place/address:** user enters a place, selects a result, map animates to it and shows the chosen place. Ask them to confirm before saving.
2. **Use my location:** request browser permission only after user action. On denial or unsupported devices, keep manual search and coordinate entry available. Do not persist precise GPS position or upload it without clear user action/consent.
3. **Enter coordinates:** latitude/longitude input with clear decimal-degree labels, validation and a preview pin.
4. **Draw boundary:** user clicks/taps points around the actual field, closes the polygon, reviews area and names the field.
5. **Import geometry:** support a defined and tested set of formats (initially GeoJSON/KML; other formats such as zipped shapefiles only after a reliable parser and validation are included). Show filenames, detected polygon count, coordinate reference assumptions, invalid features and field names before import. Never silently drop failed features.
6. **Select a detected/predefined field boundary:** only if a real provider/dataset supports it in the chosen geography. The interface must disclose how the boundaries were obtained and allow correction. Do not show selectable field outlines as though they exist if no such integration is active.

For every path, the farm name must remain the name the farmer entered. Searching an address, dropping a pin or selecting a boundary must never overwrite the farm/field name automatically.

### 29.4 Draw polygon interaction — precise sequence

1. User selects **Draw field boundary**. Enter explicit draw mode and show a short instruction (“Click around the outside edge of the field. Select the first point to close the boundary.”).
2. The first click creates vertex 1. Subsequent clicks add vertices and live connecting edges. A faint fill shows the current polygon. Show live approximate area only once enough valid vertices exist, marked “draft estimate” until closed.
3. A line preview follows the pointer/touch to the next potential vertex. Keep it subtle; never obscure satellite imagery/basemap labels.
4. Clicking the first vertex closes the polygon. A “Finish drawing” button provides a more accessible alternative. Escape cancels the drawing with a confirmation if vertices would be lost; Backspace removes only the last vertex (except when focus is in a text field); Undo removes the last geometry operation.
5. After closing, show area and perimeter in the selected unit, vertex count, field name input, and validation summary. Allow user to refine the shape before saving.
6. Dragging an existing vertex changes a draft geometry; display updated area in real time with a debounce. Keep a clear selected vertex marker and maintain pointer capture so the point does not jump.
7. Adding/removing vertices maintains valid polygon ring order. If the shape self-intersects, display the offending edges, explain why it may produce incorrect area and disable Save until fixed or corrected. Do not silently reorder vertices or change the shape.
8. If two field polygons overlap beyond the agreed tolerance, show both outlines and the estimated overlap. Offer “Review overlap,” not an automatic change. Shared borders can touch and small coordinate artefacts may be treated with a documented tolerance.
9. **Exclusion areas / holes:** allow the farmer to cut out buildings, ponds, roads or non-cultivated patches from an otherwise valid field only after explicit selection of “Add exclusion area.” Distinguish the field exterior from internal holes visually and in GeoJSON geometry. Recalculate usable area and label gross versus excluded/net area.
10. Save opens a confirm/review state summarising field name, area, parent farm, boundary edits and implications. Save must be atomic: either the valid geometry and metadata are committed together or neither is. Cancel returns to the previous persisted geometry. If there are unsaved changes and the user navigates away, ask whether to save, discard or stay.
11. After a successful saved boundary edit, create a new geometry version, recalculate derived area/centroid, invalidate affected spatial caches, and mark field-level analyses that depend on spatial coverage as stale. Keep past reports linked to the old boundary version.

### 29.5 Geometry, area and quality rules

- Use GeoJSON-compatible WGS84 coordinate storage, with a suitable geodesic/projected calculation for area and distance. Do not calculate hectares from raw latitude-longitude degree spans.
- Persist original imported geometry and normalized geometry separately where transformations are made. Store coordinate reference system when known.
- Store geometry revision, author/source, created timestamp, and edit reason where helpful.
- Validate empty polygons, fewer than three distinct vertices, duplicate adjacent vertices, invalid ring closure, self-intersection, out-of-range coordinates, unsupported geometry types, non-finite coordinates and implausible area.
- A farm may contain multiple fields. Define whether fields may overlap by design (e.g. nested management zones) or whether overlapping production fields are normally warning states. Never sum overlapping field areas into a portfolio total without a disclosed deduplication strategy.
- If a field is too small relative to a dataset pixel/grid, show the resolution mismatch and lower confidence in spatial representativeness. Do not upsample a single coarse observation into apparent field-level detail.
- If satellite images/cloud-free scenes are unavailable, keep the map usable with the best available basemap and explain the limitation. No fake NDVI/satellite raster.

### 29.6 Map layer switcher

Only list layers that are implemented and have real data available for the selected period/place. Potential layers include base map; field outlines; actual optical image; actual NDVI/EVI; rainfall; elevation/slope; soil properties; and observation-quality mask. Every layer entry has: source/provider, product name, units/legend, observation date/time window, nominal spatial resolution, processing method and quality caveat.

When the user switches layers: keep the same selected field and map extent; show layer-specific legend and loading state; handle no-data/cloud-contaminated scenes; do not change the farm boundary itself; and update the evidence metadata. If two layers cannot be meaningfully combined, disable the combination with a short reason instead of adding unreadable blended colors.

### 29.7 Map selection and map-to-page navigation

Clicking a field outline selects it and opens/updates the right inspector. A selected outline uses a clear solid accent border and restrained transparent fill; unselected fields use quieter borders and accessible labels. Clicking a field from a list flies to and selects the same geometry. “View detail” opens the exact field page. “Crop rotation” opens that field's active rotation or starts a plan if none exists. “Deep Analysis” opens the workspace preselected to that field. “Fit all fields” frames all valid field boundaries. When map edits are unsaved, changing field prompts to save/discard/stay. Preserve map selection on return from detail pages when sensible.

### 29.8 Map error and empty states

- No farm yet: show a clear empty state and “Add a farm.”
- Farm has no boundary: show the pin/coordinates and offer “Draw boundary” and “Import boundary.”
- Tile provider down: maintain editing if geometry tools can function on cached/background blank coordinates; show “Map tiles could not load” and manual coordinates/list fallback.
- Geocoder down: preserve typed query and expose manual coordinate or pan-and-select alternative.
- GPS denied: no repeated permission prompts; explain how to choose a location manually.
- Invalid geometry: highlight the exact error and the action to fix it.
- Import failure: show file-specific errors and allow retry without discarding unsaved form data.
- Partial geodata: clearly separate unavailable layers from a map rendering failure.

## 30. Page-by-page redesign contract

For every screen below, the builder must implement the named regions in the specified order. When a screen has more content than fits, use a clear hierarchy, meaningful grouping and progressive disclosure, not tiny text. Each primary button must have a real action, pending state and success/failure feedback.

### 30.1 First visit, workspace creation and empty Home

#### Quantified visual, component and state contract — First Visit / Empty Home

**Desktop composition (1440px reference):** global shell is 64px high. Under it, the main onboarding region spans the 12-column content grid with 40px side gutters and max-width 1480px. Use a photographic wide-angle aerial landscape—real crop fields, terrain and morning/late-afternoon directional light—as a hero background occupying approximately 300–360px height. Preserve realistic greens/earth colours; add a left-to-right scrim from `rgba(4,15,20,.28)` to `rgba(4,15,20,.86)` so the title sits on the darker part of the image. Never bake words into the image.

Place the title/description/primary action in a left 7-column content region, vertically centred, with 32–40px title text, 1.08 line height, weight 500–600 and a max line length of about 12–15 words. The right 5 columns may contain a restrained glass inset showing the three-step setup summary; surface `--fs-glass-strong` (86% opaque/14% transparent), 12px radius, 16px padding and 12px blur. Do not create a fake farm KPI on this screen. Under the hero, use a simple three-column process row (Location → Field details → Recommendations) with 16px section labels, 14–15px body and small line icons. It is instructional structure, not three huge cards.

**Interaction/state contract:** `Add your first farm` starts the wizard at the first incomplete required step. `Continue setup` appears only if a resumable draft exists. `Explore demo` appears only if a deterministic demo fixture exists; every value/banner/export from that workspace carries `Demo` provenance and never merges into the user's real workspace. Loading/saving state must preserve the user's intended action; errors say which draft could not be restored/saved. Creating no data must lead to an honest empty state—not seeded farm metrics.

**Engineering terms:** model this as an `onboarding state machine` (`no_workspace → draft_started → draft_in_progress → review_ready → workspace_created`). Persist wizard drafts to local IndexedDB/localStorage according to the local-first storage policy; validation should be schema-driven. The route must support reload/resume and browser back without losing valid draft answers. On completion, create a Farm entity first; create Field entities only if the user adds boundaries/fields. Do not infer a field polygon from a location pin.

**Responsive:** at 768px, reduce hero height to 280px and allow 2-column instructions; at ≤430px, hero becomes a vertical stack, title 32px, use a compact background focal crop, and primary action spans full width. The background image moves behind the title only if the scrim still satisfies contrast; otherwise use a solid dark text scrim. `prefers-reduced-motion` removes image drift.


**Purpose:** establish where the farm is and let the user get started without a giant form or a promotional landing page pretending to be the working dashboard.

**Layout:** a clean headline (“Understand your land. Plan better rotations.”), concise explanation, one primary action “Add your first farm”, secondary “Explore a clearly labelled demo farm” if a deterministic demo is implemented, and an optional “Continue setup” if a draft exists. Use a realistic agricultural image as a restrained crop/landscape backdrop only if it does not reduce contrast or consume the viewport. No fake farm metrics.

**Interaction:** “Add your first farm” opens the Add Farm wizard at the first step. “Demo farm” loads labelled illustrative data and persists an explicit demo state; demo values can never be confused with the user's real farm. “Continue setup” resumes at the correct first incomplete required step or the review page if required answers are complete. If the user has a saved farm but no current report, Home prioritises “Run your first assessment” / “Plan crop rotations” rather than onboarding cards.

### 30.2 Add Farm and Add Field wizard

#### Quantified visual, component and state contract — Add Farm / Add Field

Use a focused, step-based form shell, not a conventional dense admin form. At ≥1024px, keep the page header and progress indicator across the top, a central form column 600–720px wide, and an optional contextual map/preview region 34–40% of the available content width when the current step needs geography. Use a 3px progress track, 100% width, with a teal fill tied to actual required-step completion; optional “Sharpen accuracy” questions are excluded from the required-step denominator. Main question panel uses `--fs-glass-default` (76% opaque/24% transparent), 12px blur, 12px radius and 28–32px padding over a low-detail aerial image with a strong dark scrim. Inputs are minimum 44px high, with 8px radius, 16px text and visible focus ring.

Every step displays a 12px eyebrow (`STEP 2 OF 6 · LOCATION`), one 24–30px primary question, one 14–16px explanation of why it matters, then the relevant control. Buttons remain in a fixed footer row inside the form column: `Back` on the left, `Save & exit` as a tertiary action, and `Continue` on the right. On the final review step, use `Run analysis` or `Create farm` according to the actual workflow. The map step uses real tiles, search/geocoding with documented provider/terms, draggable location pin as a location locator, and a separate “draw boundary” tool. Do not imply a pin equals a boundary.

**Form state machine:** `draft → editing → step_valid → review → submitting → saved` or `save_failed`; each question can be `unanswered`, `answered`, `unknown/skipped`, `suggested`, or `farmer_confirmed`. No multiple-choice response is selected by default unless an actual persisted answer exists. Single-select controls must allow changing the choice; a deselect action is allowed only where the question semantics permit “unknown/none”. Inline validation includes the expected range/unit and preserves other answers. Each answer has provenance metadata (`farmer_provided`, `farmer_confirmed`, `prefilled_estimate`, `skipped`).

**Backend/data contract:** use typed `WizardDraft`, `FarmProfileInput` and `FieldBoundaryInput` DTOs; validate at client and service boundary. Prefetch may suggest relevant source-derived values but must never silently commit them. A save operation must be idempotent by draft/entity ID. Boundary changes are drafts until `Save boundary`; no analysis is triggered while the user is moving vertices. On save failure, retain the draft and geometry and provide retry. Continue/back transitions animate 180–240ms horizontal/fade; reverse direction when navigating back. Do not let motion delay keyboard action.

At ≤768px, map becomes a full-width panel above/below the question with a clear “Open map” sheet if space is tight; mobile browser back must not accidentally discard the draft. Focus moves to the new step heading after transition and returns to the invoking control on exit.


Create distinct entities: a **farm** is the overall operation and a **field** is a spatial management unit inside a farm. Do not use “farm” and “field” interchangeably in labels or calculations.

Farm wizard steps: (1) name and optional note; (2) country/region/location search, map pin or coordinate entry; (3) draw/import farm boundary or save location and add boundary later; (4) add fields now or later; (5) review location, area, selected units and data/service limitations; (6) save.

Field wizard steps: (1) field name; (2) boundary draw/import/select supported field outline; (3) optional crop in ground and planting date; (4) soil/water knowledge—known, estimated or not known; (5) crop history; (6) review and save.

All steps: autosave locally as drafts; show stage counter; persistent Back; Save & exit; optional Skip only where allowed; validate inputs inline; keep name values independent of map search; no surprise overwrite of existing fields; review all collected information before creation. If creation partially fails, the UI reports what was saved and resumes safely without duplicate farms/fields on retry.

### 30.3 Home — daily farm tracker

#### Quantified visual and interaction contract — Home

**Reference composition:** build the page as a full-width dashboard with a cinematic agricultural hero strip, then a dense but calm operational grid. At 1440px, use 36–40px horizontal gutters, a max-width 1480px shell, and a 280–320px hero region below the 64px top bar. Use a realistic aerial field/landscape photograph with the visual focal point toward the right or upper half; apply a 0.25–0.35 opacity deep-navy tint plus a left-side dark scrim that reaches roughly 86% opacity behind text. Keep actual title and metrics in the DOM above the image layer. Do not use a storm-only image as unrelated ornament: the environment should signal real agricultural land and adapt only where a real weather state is available.

In the hero's left 7 columns place a short contextual greeting, title `Smarter decisions. Healthier fields.` (or equivalent approved final copy), a one-sentence summary and primary `Plan crop rotations` action. In the right 5 columns show a compact `Current Conditions` glass surface (`--fs-glass-default`, 76% opaque, 12px blur, 12px radius, 20px padding) with current/recent temperature, rain observation and source timestamp. Do not place fine text directly on a high-detail photo. Below hero, arrange a 12-column grid: Farm Overview spans 4 columns, Current Conditions / Weather Detail spans 3–4 columns if not already in hero, and Priority Actions spans 4–5 columns. Use one clear section heading above the grid, cards 12px radius, 20px padding, and 16–20px gaps. Section headings 18–20px; card headings 15–17px; primary values 24–32px; body 14–15px; metadata 12–13px.

A lower “Recent insights / What changed” region is a horizontal list of at most three meaningful items; use a left icon/status marker, compact title, one-line evidence-backed summary, and explicit timestamp/context. Each item routes to its exact field, observation or report. Portfolio Watch appears only if another farm needs attention; it identifies that farm by name and provides `View farm` / `Switch farm`. Do not display empty alert containers or dummy trends. Advanced telemetry lives in a collapsed/secondary area or links into Intelligence rather than crowding the primary status card.

**State/data dependencies:** selected farm is canonical global state; every card derives from the same `FarmStatusAssessment` and selected farm ID. On farm switch, set the selection first, cancel/ignore in-flight responses for the previous farm, then hydrate matching data; never flash mixed-farm metrics. Status card states are `loading`, `current`, `stale`, `partial`, `unavailable`, `error`. If a prior report exists but input/source dependencies changed, preserve the report but show a visible stale banner and `Review / rerun` action. If no report exists, show `Plan crop rotations` and `Run Deep Analysis` only when their prerequisites are clear.

The weather card must distinguish `Observed/reanalysis`, `Current conditions` and `Forecast`; include a timestamp and exact period for rainfall. The Next Action component is driven by an action/risk rule with evidence references; if none applies, say no immediate evidence-backed action was identified, but only if the monitored inputs are sufficient. Do not use decorative status curves to imply a measured trend unless the underlying time series exists. For a trend sparkline, include its date window and metric label.

At 1280px the grid can change span but remains a 12-column system; at 768px stack hero content and use 2-column cards; at ≤430px stack cards one column and keep the primary next action above secondary telemetry. The hero background uses `background-size: cover` with a documented focal position, a separate scrim and `pointer-events:none` image layers. Hover elevations are 1–2px maximum; entrance motion 220ms. Respect reduced-motion.


**Page hierarchy:**

1. Page title and compact selected-farm selector, with “Add farm” / “View all farms” secondary actions.
2. A main status panel showing farm name, location, current condition label (Good / Needs attention / Alert / Insufficient data), last evidence update time, active crop and plan phase if known.
3. Current conditions/weather card with current/recent values or forecast if a real forecast source is connected; always label observation, forecast and reanalysis distinctly. Show a short range or context such as “rainfall below typical for this period” only when the baseline is computed.
4. Crop rotation at a glance: crop photo, current crop, sequence, season/year, next transition window, days until transition only if dates are supported, and a clickable sequence leading to the correct field/rotation.
5. Water Intelligence summary: plain-language water balance/status, supported recent precipitation, modelled crop demand and data freshness; advanced detail opens Water Intelligence.
6. Soil Intelligence summary: a supported soil property or condition indicator and whether the value is a lab measurement, mapped estimate or calculation. Advanced detail opens Soil & Land.
7. “What changed since your last check?” short ranked events, only if comparison evidence exists.
8. Recommended next action(s), with reason, due window and action state. If nothing is justified, say “No immediate action identified from the available data.”
9. Active alerts only. Do not reserve an empty alert block; no critical status from a silent fallback.
10. Optional portfolio watch when multiple farms/fields exist: list each relevant field and its specific condition.

**Click reactions:** farm selector updates all sections together; crop sequence opens the matching rotation stage; weather values open their evidence; water/soil summaries open detail tabs; alert opens evidence and affected field; action opens task detail; report history opens dated report. “Start New Analysis” should not be the same action as Deep Analysis or rotation planning; use labels “Plan Crop Rotations” and “Run Deep Analysis” as context-appropriate actions.

**Simple mode:** short status sentences, 1–3 actions and the key crop/rotation view. **Advanced mode:** source timestamps, telemetry, supported 30-day trends, water/soil detail, sensor resolution, scientific explanations and trend thresholds. Keep Advanced content expandable rather than dumping every variable onto Home.

### 30.4 My Farms portfolio page

#### Quantified visual and component contract — My Farms

Use a portfolio workspace, not a set of identical oversized cards. At 1440px the content shell uses 36–40px gutters. Page heading and subtitle sit at top left; `Search farms…`, view switch if implemented, and `+ Add farm` align on the top-right baseline. Beneath them, use a compact portfolio-summary strip (only values supported by saved farms) with 3–4 metrics such as total mapped area, farms, fields and active alerts; each metric is a label/value pair, not a separate giant tile. Then a full-width data table/list with columns `Farm / Location`, `Mapped area`, `Fields`, `Current plan`, `Status`, `Last assessment`, and row actions. Use 56–64px row height minimum, 12–14px column labels, 14–15px row text, right-aligned numeric columns and tabular numerals. Use real thumbnail imagery only if a reliable farm image or map snapshot exists; don't use the same stock image to imply different farms are real. A subtle selected-row tint (`rgba(39,199,178,.08)`) is sufficient; table container uses solid `--fs-surface-solid` or `--fs-glass-strong` depending on backdrop.

Clicking a row's main area opens the selected farm detail workspace; the overflow menu contains `Rename`, `Manage fields`, `Open map`, `Run Deep Analysis` (when valid), `Archive` or `Delete` with explicit confirmation. Each action has its own domain handler; no menu item is decorative. Search filters only the user's accessible farm list and includes no-match/reset states. Sort controls show active field/direction and are keyboard operable. Multi-select/bulk actions are not included unless their consequences and confirm dialog are specified.

Advanced mode may add portfolio rollups, risk distribution and comparative table columns, but averages are permitted only across comparable fields with the same metric meaning, time range, unit, depth/source semantics and method version. If non-comparable, show per-field values and coverage instead of one misleading average. Status is derived from the shared assessment object, not a separate hard-coded badge.

**Data and state:** empty farms → onboarding CTA; farms with no analysis → `Analysis not run`; stale report → `Needs refresh`; partial source data → `Partial`; complete fresh result → status derived from rules. Rename uses optimistic UI only if rollback is implemented and the persistence write is confirmed. Delete uses a confirmation dialog with farm name and downstream-data consequences; if backend schedules exist, deletion/archival requires schedule suspension and policy-defined report handling. Local-only mode must state deletion only clears this browser's data; cross-device persistence must never be implied.

Responsive: desktop table; tablet hides lower-priority columns behind a `Details` disclosure; mobile uses compact farm rows with a 2-line identity block and clear status, with row actions in a labelled menu. Do not force whole-page horizontal scrolling.


**Page hierarchy:** page title + subtitle, search, status filters, sort, list/table toggle if useful, “Add farm” primary action, then farm table/list. Do not surround the table with multiple nested cards.

Desktop columns: farm thumbnail/mark, farm name and location, total boundary area and number of fields, current crop-plan state, farm status, last analysis with freshness, active alert count, and a row action menu. Values are sourced from records; avoid hardcoded sample farms in a real account. On mobile, each farm becomes a compact stacked row with status and key actions; it must not overflow horizontally.

**Actions:** open farm; rename; edit farm information; manage fields/map; duplicate configuration only with a clear explanation that data/history are not duplicated unless selected; archive; delete with consequence summary and confirmation; export. Search must search names and location labels. Sorting/filtering must operate on actual records.

**Click behaviour:** clicking a farm row opens that farm's Overview, not a generic page. The active/selected farm is reflected in global state. Deletion checks linked fields/plans/reports; explain what will be removed and whether records in cloud/backups are also deleted. If deletion is irreversible, do not claim it is undoable.

Advanced-only portfolio information can include total managed area, active farms, fields by status, rotation reviews due and alerts. Do not compute an “average health” figure from incompatible or missing component metrics without a documented aggregation model.

### 30.5 Farm detail workspace

#### Quantified visual and information-architecture contract — Farm / Field Detail

Use a master-detail workspace with a stable left contextual rail and a larger main content pane. At ≥1280px the left rail is 240–260px wide; main pane fills remaining width. The rail has a compact farm cover image (16:9 crop, about 120–150px high), farm name, location, mapped area and an active/needs-attention status. Below, use a vertical navigation list with 40–44px row height: Overview, Fields & Map, Rotation Plan, Soil & Land, Water Intelligence, Weather & Climate, Past Analysis, Journal, Farm Settings. Only one active section gets a 3px accent edge and restrained selected fill (teal alpha 0.08); no neon outline. The data content starts with a breadcrumb and section title, then an optional compact field selector if the section is field scoped.

The main pane uses a 12-column responsive grid with 20–24px gaps. Overview reproduces Home's canonical status data but expanded; avoid copying every Home card verbatim. The rotation section uses the canonical full-width rotation card/timeline; soil/water sections use charts with axes/units/source captions and tables for inspectable values. Image assets have a clear purpose: real crop photos for crop-stage cards, real satellite imagery in maps, contextual farmland photography for small profile headers only. Do not use cinematic photo behind data tables if it makes values harder to read.

**State/context:** `farmId`, `fieldId`, `activeSection`, selected rotation ID and crop-focus ID must remain in route context. Navigating away and back restores section/field/scroll context where practical. If clicking a crop from Calendar or a report, the route opens the exact field and rotation stage with a focus banner; clear the focus only on explicit close or selection change. Editing a farm profile creates a new profile version; it does not rewrite previous report inputs. A change that affects analysis creates a stale flag on affected outputs and displays which dependencies changed.

**Backend/domain:** detail panels should call selectors over the canonical farm/field records and related analysis reports; do not re-query each card independently with different IDs/time ranges. Use a query/cache key including farmId, fieldId, section, reportId and data window. Invalidation rules are declared in Section 43. Save states need pending/saved/failed feedback. Destructive delete/archive follows the portfolio contract.

At 1024px the rail collapses to a dropdown/compact secondary nav; at 768px becomes a tab strip or drawer; at ≤430px single-column with field selector at top. All section navigation is keyboard accessible and does not rely on hover.


Use a consistent farm heading region with: breadcrumb `My Farms > [Farm]`; farm name; location and area; status with text; last analysis; actions menu; field/farm selector when needed. A map thumbnail/mini-map can sit beside the heading, but it must be clickable to open the full boundary workspace.

Desktop sub-navigation can be a compact left rail inside the farm workspace. On tablet/mobile convert it to a labelled horizontal scroll menu or dropdown. Do not copy an oversized sidebar onto all global pages.

Required sections:

- **Overview / Farm Tracker:** summary from Home with expanded details.
- **Fields & Map:** fields list, boundary editor, create/import field, selected field status and comparison.
- **Crop Rotation Plan:** active rotation for selected field; if multiple fields, make the field selector explicit and do not combine them into one sequence.
- **Soil & Land:** properties table, texture composition chart, pH/reference ranges only where relevant, slope/erosion context, depth and source; lab measurements take precedence only when appropriate and current.
- **Water Intelligence:** crop water demand, rainfall/irrigation contributions, net water balance, model assumptions, data history and calculation explorer.
- **Weather & Climate:** actual current forecast from an identified source, historical weather/reanalysis with timestamps, climate baseline and risks by crop window.
- **Vegetation & Field Change:** satellite imagery or vegetation index only when genuinely retrieved and quality-screened; display scene date, sensor, resolution and cloud/no-data states.
- **Actions & Observations:** planned/done/overdue actions, farmer log and record details.
- **Past Analyses:** report list, dated scores and comparison.
- **Farm Settings:** editable farm details, unit preferences where global, default practices, data permissions and deletion/archive.

Each section starts with a human-readable summary then offers advanced expansion. Standard rows and charts link to evidence. Do not duplicate different copies of the same metric with conflicting formulas.

### 30.6 Crop Rotation Planner — question wizard

#### Quantified visual, form, and calculation contract — Rotation Wizard

The planner wizard must feel like a focused decision workflow, not a chat clone or a generic survey. Use a two-region desktop layout where appropriate: primary question panel at 58–66% width and a contextual “what this changes” panel at 34–42% width. On the location/boundary step the map may become the dominant region; for non-geographic questions, the progress and form are the focal point. Form surface: `--fs-glass-default` (76% opaque), 12px blur, 12px radius and 28px padding. Context/preview panel: `--fs-glass-strong` (86% opaque), 8px blur, 12px radius and 20px padding. Title 26–30px, prompt body 15–16px, answer labels 14–15px, helper metadata 12–13px. Inputs minimum 44px high, focus border 2px teal, selected answer background `rgba(39,199,178,.10)` with a 1px selected border; unselected answers must not look pre-selected.

One question occupies one step unless the question truly requires a small set of dependent fields. Show required-step progress as `Question 3 of 6 · Soil` and keep optional data in a later `Improve accuracy` stage. Every question has a `Why we ask` explanation and an `How this affects the result` disclosure. Unknown/skipped is a legitimate state only if the field permits it; if the field is required, explain why it must be supplied. Changing an upstream answer revalidates only dependent answers and flags those now inconsistent on the review screen.

The right preview may display selected priorities and a small, clearly labelled provisional summary, but must not present a final rotation score before the model finishes. Priority sliders use a fixed defined range and show value text, not only a colored handle. If weights are normalised, show the final effective weights in Advanced Mode and make the anti-domination constraint transparent.

**Typed data and state:** persist a `RotationPlanDraft` with version, answers, provenance/confirmation statuses and current step. Use a deterministic schema to validate soil, water, crop history, horizon and priorities. AI parsing of natural language can suggest structured input only where the wizard includes such a field; it must preserve original text, flag ambiguity and require farmer confirmation. `Run analysis` captures immutable input snapshot/hash; during submission, prevent duplicate double-click submissions with an idempotency key. Errors preserve form state and point to the failed step/source. `Back`/browser history must not submit or wipe data.

The progress screen is not a decorative animation: it consumes real pipeline progress events. In a browser-only/local calculation, represent known synchronous stages honestly; when server-side, use job polling/SSE and current job ID. A cancelled run must stop/ignore downstream responses and leave the draft available. Motion between wizard stages 200–280ms, with reversed direction for Back; reduced motion removes slide transitions.


The planner is the primary tool for “Which rotations fit this field?” It is not Deep Analysis and it is not a What-If scenario.

Wizard contract:

- One decision/question per stage; use conditional follow-up only when it changes the model.
- Header: breadcrumb/context, progress text (“Step 3 of 7 required”), progress bar and Save & exit.
- Main column: clear question (32–48px desktop, responsive), helper line explaining why the question matters, exactly one principal input group and one primary action.
- Side rail on desktop may list broad categories, not future unanswered questions with confusing status.
- Back is always available; keyboard Back is supported without stealing browser shortcuts from text inputs. Next/Continue is disabled only when an answer is genuinely required and invalid.
- Single-choice cards can auto-advance only after a safe short delay and cancellation route; prefer explicit Continue for consequential or ambiguous inputs.
- All drafts save locally on every answer and sync only according to consent/identity architecture. The UI says Saved, Saving or Save failed honestly.
- Optional unknowns use “I don't know” and “Skip this question”; these are explicit states, not an empty string.
- Every input has an accessible label and correct units.

**Required question groups:** field/location selection; soil texture/known soil info; water source and irrigation type/reliability; crop history; planning horizon and cropping intensity; priorities. Optional accuracy group: pH/lab data, drainage/slope/tillage, pest/disease history, fertilizer/residue practices, crop exclusions/inclusions, water salinity, yields, available planting windows and any other factor the actual model supports.

The review screen shows all inputs grouped under Farm/Field, Soil, Water, Crop History, Management, Planning and Priorities. Each row shows value, units, provenance, last-confirmed date and inline Edit. Editing an upstream value identifies which later answers become irrelevant, which analysis cache is invalidated and why.

### 30.7 Crop Rotation Planner — result and detail pages

#### Quantified visual, sequence-card, and comparison contract — Rotation Results

The recommended rotation is the dominant result. At 1440px, render one wide 12-column recommendation panel with three internal zones divided by two neutral hairline rules: score/decision summary 22–25%, crop sequence 48–54%, and reason/benefits summary 23–28%. Target a panel height of roughly 250–310px according to crop image ratio, 12px radius, 24–28px padding and background `--fs-glass-strong` (86% opaque) if photo-backed, or solid `--fs-surface-solid` on plain canvas. Do not create a 5:1 strip so compressed that crop names/metadata become unreadable. Crop cards are photo-led: each has a 4:3 or square image, clear Year label, separately labelled Season, crop name, family tag, duration chip (e.g. `~115 days in field`) and a contextual expand control. Never combine Year and Season into one ambiguous term. Crop family uses label/text in addition to color. Images must correspond to the actual crop; no random plant photo pretending to be another species.

Score zone: display `Match score` with `/100`, explicit meaning and confidence separately. Include 3–4 small component metrics only where computed (e.g. water performance, soil outcome, resilience, productivity); each component has a label and value with units/meaning. The center sequence uses arrows with enough space and a timeline continuation rule if more crops than fit. The right zone includes up to 3 compact strengths and 1–3 most material risks; don't hide drawbacks beneath a generic “Why this works”. Buttons: `View full plan`, `Compare`, `Select this plan` (if selection is supported), and AI explanation action only if a real backend response can be generated. Every button has a defined destination/state effect.

Below the hero: (1) current farm/field context strip, (2) season-by-season plan table/timeline, (3) Benefits & Risks with mitigation, (4) alternatives sorted by score with stable candidate IDs and reason for ranking, (5) crop suitability, (6) Evidence / methodology drawer. Only 3–4 sections are expanded by default; use accordions/tabs to manage density. Avoid nested card stacks. Advanced Mode reveals all supported component values, formulas, assumptions, model versions, source references and soil/N/water trajectories; Simple Mode shows summary values and plain-language trade-offs from the same calculation result.

**Ranking/domain rules:** hard constraints determine candidate feasibility before weighted preference ranking. User priority weights may alter the aggregate objective but do not alter raw component metrics. A rotation failing a safety/feasibility floor must be marked infeasible or clearly downgraded with the exact reason; it must not be forced to #1 by preference weights. Candidate IDs are stable and independent of display names. Same input snapshot + engine/crop-KB version + seed must reproduce identical ranking. Ties require deterministic tie-break rules. Confidence is separate from match score. No score or percent uplift can be displayed unless the calculation and baseline are defined.

When selected, the plan becomes the canonical active selected plan for that field only after user confirmation. Update My Farms, Home, Calendar and Simulator baseline through one versioned event; preserve old plan and report history. If re-ranking occurs, never silently swap the farmer's selected plan. The `selected` state and `recommended` state are distinct.


Result page order:

1. field/farm context and analysis timestamp;
2. best-ranked valid rotation summary;
3. one wide three-part rotation card: score and headline trade-offs on the left, crop sequence in the centre, why-it-fits summary on the right;
4. priority/component score summary and evidence confidence (not conflated);
5. Current field context: current crop, plan stage, next transition, supported water/soil signals;
6. Benefits & Risks, including risk level, affected crop/stage, evidence or mechanism, mitigation and what is uncertain;
7. Ranked alternatives and a working compare selector;
8. schedule/timeline and review trigger;
9. additional supporting insights; deep details remain collapsible.

**Rotation sequence cards:** use realistic crop photography, crop name below image, year/season labels above, arrows or a visible directional link between stages, duration and planting/harvest windows where supported. Hover/tap displays duration in the field, botanical family and relevant stage note. On mobile, use a vertical or horizontally scrollable sequence region with clear control; never make the whole page overflow.

**Scoring and plan labels:** describe the overall figure as a match/suitability score out of 100, not predicted yield or likelihood of success. Display component scores (suitability, water, soil health, resilience, productivity, diversity) only when each is defined, computable and tested. Every metric has a definition, basis, formula/version in advanced detail, source/provenance, period and confidence where applicable.

**Ranked candidates:** show top 3 expanded initially; remaining candidates are compact and expandable or paginated. Advanced mode can expose the full valid ranked set with filters. Add a “Not recommended under current assumptions” group only for actual candidates tested and rejected, including the hard constraint or factor causing rejection. Do not call a plan “valid” if it violates crop-family return intervals, growing windows or required water constraints.

**Head-to-head comparison:** farmer selects two rotations (three only if layout remains readable). Display score, duration, crop sequence, each priority component, water demand/availability, soil trajectory, nitrogen assumptions, crop-family break, disease-cycle considerations, seasonal climate fit, workload/timing and evidence/confidence. At the bottom give an explicit conditional conclusion: “Plan A is the stronger match for your current water-saving priority; Plan B scores better on productivity potential under the stated assumptions.” Show when the difference is too small or unsupported to justify a confident winner.

**Detailed rotation screen:** selected plan title and active/saved status; crop sequence; year vs season distinction; duration explanation; planting and harvest windows; crop-specific climate conditions; risks/mitigations; water demand vs effective rainfall and reported irrigation; soil and nitrogen/organic matter trajectory where the model is validated; review-by date and trigger; source and assumptions drawer; print/export one-page field plan. Every button must work.

### 30.8 Farm Intelligence / Deep Analysis workspace

#### Quantified visual and telemetry contract — Farm Intelligence

This is an assessment and monitoring workspace, not a second Home page. At 1440px use a 12-column grid: primary summary/priority zone spans 4 columns; assessment timeline and high-level trend may span 8 columns below or beside it according to the active subview. The page header shows selected farm, field scope (`All fields` or one field), current data time, prior report date, freshness indicator and `Run Deep Analysis`. The primary score/condition panel uses 22–28px for the score and a 16–18px status headline; definition, range and confidence are directly adjacent. Use `--fs-glass-default` (76% opaque, 12px blur) for photo-backed summary cards; use solid/strong surfaces for data tables and evidence lists. Any header photograph is a shallow (160–220px) field-context strip, not a full-page obstructive wallpaper.

The workspace's visual sequence is: **current assessment → highest-priority findings → field-by-field summary → change since previous report → recommended actions → data coverage / methodology**. Render findings as a ranked list with severity icon + text, evidence phrase, date/window, affected field and next action. Use no more than four major sections expanded initially. Keep a chart title + one-sentence takeaway above each chart. Charts have axes, units, selected time range, legend and source status; every chart has a text/table alternative. Low/medium/high risk color must also have labels and icons.

The `Run Deep Analysis` button opens a run panel summarising the scope, refreshable data sources and stored practice routine. The user can review/edit natural-language routine inputs before initiating. The run's progress UI uses actual job stages (profile snapshot, connector refresh, validation, calculations, field assessments, report assembly), not timers. Partial sources produce a partial report with missing inputs highlighted. If there is no prior report, say “First assessment” rather than inventing a trend.

**State / backend:** `idle → confirming_inputs → queued/running → complete|partial|failed|cancelled`. Store a run ID, immutable input snapshot, source outcome list and model version. Repeated double-clicks must map to one idempotent job. On completion, fetch the returned report ID and route to the report view. On failed job, preserve its safe diagnostic details and expose `Retry` only if retryable. Farm-wide score aggregates field assessments only under defined comparable weighting rules; always show field-level outcomes beside it so one severe field cannot disappear into an average. Schedule settings are managed separately from manual analysis but shown as a status summary here.


**Purpose:** “How are the selected farm and its fields doing, what changed, what risks need attention, and which improvements deserve consideration?” It is a monitoring/diagnostic workflow, not a replacement for rotation planning.

Header: `Farm Intelligence`; selected farm; field scope (`All fields` or selected subset); latest report timestamp; current assessment freshness; primary `Run Deep Analysis` action; schedule status/menu; reports/history link. Show a compact evidence coverage strip and a warning if critical sources are missing or stale.

Before running, open a short preflight panel: selected scope; relevant profile/input changes; data sources expected; last confirmed irrigation/management routine; whether the user wants to update it now. Offer “Use last confirmed routine” with its timestamp and “Update practices.” Do not silently assume the stored routine is current.

Run state sequence:

1. Validate selected farm/field IDs, boundaries, profile completeness and permission to run.
2. Freeze a run-input snapshot (farm/field profile versions, crop plan versions, practice records, data time windows, sources, model/crop KB versions).
3. Queue/execute required data refresh jobs with per-source timeout, cache and retry policy.
4. Validate each response and unit; handle data gaps/fill values; check spatial and temporal adequacy; store provenance.
5. Calculate supported field-level indicators and field assessments.
6. Calculate a farm-wide summary using the declared aggregation methodology; show field scores separately and never allow aggregation to obscure severe field-specific alerts.
7. Generate evidence-linked findings and actions through structured templates/rules. AI may paraphrase and parse user language, but cannot fabricate measurements or invent causes.
8. Validate each generated explanatory claim against structured calculations and sources. If validation fails, omit the claim or mark it unverified.
9. Persist a dated immutable report and update current-report pointer only after successful finalisation.
10. Display report status, coverage, partial failures and “what changed since the previous report.”

### 30.9 Deep Analysis report — full hierarchy

#### Quantified report layout and evidence hierarchy — Deep Analysis Report

Treat the report as a readable diagnostic document, not a collection of dashboard tiles. At desktop width, use a 12-column content grid with approximately 900–1120px readable report measure; a compact right-side contents rail (220–260px) may appear above 1280px, while the main report body occupies the rest. Keep the title, farm/field scope, report date, overall status, evidence confidence and input snapshot/version in the opening region. A shallow real agricultural image may be used as a header background only if a dark scrim secures contrast; data-heavy content belongs on solid or 86%-opaque surfaces. Hero summary headline 30–36px; section headings 20–24px; prose 15–16px; metadata 12–13px; table text at least 13–14px.

The immutable report order is: (1) Assessment summary and overall farm state; (2) field-by-field score/status table; (3) most important findings with severity, evidence and caveat; (4) water and soil status; (5) crop/rotation implications; (6) recommended actions with urgency and due windows; (7) changes since prior report; (8) data gaps and freshness; (9) method, formula, source and version appendix. For each finding, place evidence and uncertainty immediately beside the conclusion. Example: `Root-zone moisture estimate is below the selected baseline` followed by `Source: [product], depth: [basis], period: [dates], coverage/confidence: [value]`. Do not phrase a vegetation-index drop as confirmed disease.

Each numeric metric exposes a value, unit, date/time basis, source type and status. Selecting `Why?` opens the Evidence Drawer with observed/input/modelled/calculated classification, input values, formula and version. Report export (if implemented) uses the same immutable report data and layout, not a screenshot and not a fresh recalculation at export time. `Print` uses a light print stylesheet but preserves labels, sources and caveats.

**State/data contract:** reports are immutable snapshots; edits create new input versions and new reports, never overwrite old values. Report DTO includes a compatibility/version marker. Missing values appear as `Unavailable — [specific reason]`, not zero or blanks that imply zero. If no comparable previous report exists, hide deltas and show the reason. A report's status is computed from the assessment result and per-field data, with a separate `data confidence` measure. The report may be `complete`, `partial`, or `unavailable`; do not badge a partial report as complete.

Keyboard focus for expandable evidence must return to the triggering button when closed. A sticky contents rail must account for the 64px global header and never cover headings; mobile uses a `Report sections` disclosure instead.


1. **Report header:** farm name; date and timezone; scope and fields included; run status (Complete / Partial / Failed); model/version; data window; evidence freshness.
2. **Farm condition summary:** farm-wide score/index only if validated; otherwise a summary of supported component indicators. Explanation, confidence and aggregation method.
3. **Field-by-field results:** each field's condition, separate score/indicators, top finding, alert severity, evidence quality and link to field detail.
4. **Priority findings:** sorted by severity, confidence, urgency and actionability, not just by visual drama. Each includes “What we noticed”, “Why it matters”, evidence, date/range, uncertainty and “What to do next.”
5. **Water intelligence:** precipitation period and source; forecast/reanalysis/observation distinction; estimated crop demand and irrigation records if supported; water-balance method; missing practice data; limitations.
6. **Soil and land:** available properties, depth, source, measured vs mapped distinction, drainage/slope context, missing lab measurements and confidence.
7. **Weather/climate exposure:** heat/dry spell/frost/flooding exposure only with crop-specific thresholds and relevant time windows; forecasts separate from long-term baseline.
8. **Vegetation and field change:** only real retrieved imagery/index values; scene dates; cloud quality; spatial-resolution explanation; do not infer exact disease/nutrient diagnosis from greenness alone.
9. **Management-practice context:** user-confirmed irrigation, planting, fertiliser, residue and scouting history; mark unknown or stale routines visibly.
10. **Potential risks:** risk level, causal mechanism or plausible pathways, evidence supporting it, confidence and mitigation. Distinguish “possible risk” from confirmed event.
11. **Recommended actions:** priority, reason, field, due window, dependencies, estimated effort only if supported, action buttons and evidence link.
12. **Data gaps and improvement opportunities:** missing inputs and how to provide them; predicted effect on reliability expressed qualitatively unless validated quantitatively.
13. **Change comparison:** compare with previous comparable report; split difference drivers into changed farmer inputs, new observation/data, changed geometry/crop plan, different model version or missing-data change.
14. **Methods and provenance:** expandable full source registry, variable values and units, timestamps, calculation steps, thresholds, assumptions, model version and quality flags.
15. **Run summary and audit:** stage outcomes, source failures, partial computations, run ID and report creation timestamp. Never expose secret tokens or unnecessary sensitive logs.

The report must distinguish **farm condition**, **evidence confidence**, **data readiness** and **recommendation priority** as different concepts. A single composite farm health score is not mandatory if it cannot be scientifically justified. The requirement is that both farm-wide and field-level views exist; the farm-wide view can summarise component metrics until a defensible validated composite is available.

### 30.10 Report history and comparison

#### Quantified history table and comparison contract

Use a report-history workspace with two clearly separated areas: a chronological history list/table and an explicit comparison selector. Desktop layout: primary list 7–8 columns, selected comparison summary 4–5 columns; if a chart is used, it spans the content width below the selected reports. Each history row includes report date/time, farm/field scope, trigger (manual/scheduled), status (complete/partial/failed), farm and field score where comparable, confidence, report version and an `Open` action. Use 60–72px rows, 14px primary text and 12–13px metadata. Filters include time range, field and run status. Do not build one large card for each historical report if a table is clearer.

Comparison mode requires selecting two immutable reports. The sticky comparison header shows both report IDs/dates, selected farm/field and a `Comparable / Partially comparable / Not comparable` state. Metric delta rows include previous value, current value, delta with units, direction meaning, confidence and reason for comparability. Green cannot universally mean “higher is better”; use metric-specific direction metadata. If engine/model/crop-KB versions differ, either recompute under a common version or label the comparison as method-changed and avoid treating raw score delta as direct improvement. Missing data in one report is a coverage change, not a zero delta.

**Backend:** endpoint receives report IDs and scope; server returns a comparison DTO with explicit comparability flags and metric-level reasons. Historical reports remain immutable. Generate `Why did this change?` categories from actual input/data/source/model-version diffs; label the result as an attribution/explanation rather than causal proof. Loading uses content-shaped skeleton rows; errors preserve the selected report IDs and permit retry.


Report history is a dated list or timeline per farm, with field filters and clear Complete/Partial/Failed states. Every report is immutable after creation except for an added annotation/correction record. A new run creates a new report; it does not rewrite an old one.

Selecting two reports opens a comparison with: farm and field scope differences; scores only where comparable; changed inputs; data source/product/timestamp differences; model/crop database version differences; metric-by-metric changes; new/resolved alerts; recommended actions added/completed/deferred; and missing values introduced by failed sources. If a method changes, show “not directly comparable” or recompute both under a common method when valid source data is available.

Allow export to PDF/CSV after confirming its scope; include a report ID, generated date, source caveats and model version. Do not include more precise location detail than appropriate for the selected report-sharing/privacy setting.

### 30.11 Automation and schedule settings

#### Quantified automation UI and job-state contract

Automation settings use a clear schedule-detail page with a 12-column desktop layout: schedule form/controls occupy 7 columns; right-side `What will be sent/stored` and `Last run` summary occupies 5 columns. Do not make schedule management look like a simple on/off toggle with hidden consequences. A schedule panel uses `--fs-glass-strong` (86% opaque) or a solid surface, 20–24px padding, 12px radius. Inputs are 44px high. Each schedule card shows scope (farm/field), cadence and timezone, next run, last outcome, consent/snapshot status and an explicit enabled/paused/needs-attention label.

Schedule cadence uses allowed choices such as daily, every 3 days, weekly, and custom only where supported. Show the calculated next run timestamp in the farm/workspace timezone. When first enabling, use a stepwise confirmation: (1) select scope/cadence; (2) review the practice snapshot and freshness needs; (3) disclose the exact fields that leave the device and what is stored remotely; (4) define reporting/notifications; (5) confirm consent. Explain that a backend cannot access the browser's localStorage while it is closed unless the required data snapshot has been explicitly transmitted and authenticated.

**Job state machine:** `draft → awaiting_consent → enabled → queued → running → complete|partial|failed → retrying|paused|disabled`. State label, buttons and explanatory text are derived from the job/schedule record. `Pause` stops future schedule occurrences; it does not erase reports. `Disable` requires a confirmation and revokes future runs. `Run now` creates an idempotent manual job for the same scope, with deduplication if a matching job is already running. Each run row includes start/end timestamp, job ID, source outcome summary, retry count and report link. Failed source calls are disclosed in the run result; no green success toast unless the job service returns a successful status.

**Backend contract:** schedule create/update/delete endpoints validate cadence/timezone/scope; scheduler claims due jobs through a durable queue with lease/lock and idempotency key; job processor reads only the consented minimum snapshot; connector adapters have timeouts and bounded retries with exponential backoff and jitter. Store immutable report output and safe structured logs with a correlation ID; never log API keys, original free-text practice if not needed, or sensitive farm coordinates in generic logs. A missing/expired snapshot moves the schedule to `needs sync` or `suspended`; it must not analyze stale browser values as though current. Rainfall observations never prove irrigation did not occur.

Responsive layouts stack consent and schedule summary; destructive disable/revoke actions require deliberate confirmation and clearly describe effect. Accessible screen-reader announcements report queued/running/completed/failed status.


Automation setup uses a clearly separated flow:

1. Choose farm and fields.
2. Choose frequency: daily, every 3 days, weekly or a custom supported schedule. Explain that the schedule is a re-evaluation frequency, not a promise that every source publishes new observations at that cadence.
3. Choose preferred run window and timezone derived from farm/user settings. Handle daylight-saving transitions correctly where relevant.
4. Review which inputs will be sent to the backend for the scheduled job, why they are required, how long they are retained, where deletion applies and what stays local.
5. Confirm the last known irrigation/management routine. Ask whether it remains valid. If unchanged, record confirmation timestamp. If changed, capture new inputs and preserve the previous value as historical.
6. Configure material-change notifications, ordinary report-ready notices and repeated-alert suppression.
7. Review and explicitly enable schedule.

**Scheduled run logic:** a backend scheduler enqueues jobs only for enabled and consented schedules. It loads the minimum permitted snapshot; refreshes eligible sources; skips data-dependent recomputation if relevant inputs/data have not changed and policy permits; otherwise runs the analysis; stores source statuses and report; and sends notification only according to user settings. If cloud input is missing or consent expired, do not improvise with local data the backend cannot read—mark the job as waiting for updated device sync or suspended and explain it.

**Rainfall rule:** only use newly available rainfall observations/reanalysis from an appropriate source and mark their type/date. Forecast rain may influence forward-looking planning but is not recorded as observed rainfall. Rainfall does not prove irrigation did not happen. Unless the farmer records an irrigation event or a supported sensor establishes it, irrigation remains unknown/reported routine; it is not automatically subtracted from the record based only on rainfall.

**Schedule lifecycle:** Draft → Awaiting consent → Enabled → Queued → Running → Complete/Partial/Failed → Retrying (if eligible) → Paused/Suspended/Disabled. Preserve prior runs. Include `Run now`, `Pause`, `Resume`, `Change schedule`, `View last report` and `Disable` actions. Confirm disabling and explain that existing reports remain. `Run now` must respect per-user/field rate limits and show whether it queues or executes immediately.

**Idempotency and duplicates:** one logical schedule occurrence generates at most one final report per scope and input snapshot unless user explicitly reruns. Retries reuse a job ID/idempotency key. Concurrent runs for the same farm/field/window are de-duplicated or safely serialized.

### 30.12 Notification centre

#### Quantified notification drawer and status model

Desktop notification centre opens from the global bell as a right-side drawer 380–440px wide, or a full notification route for history/filters. Drawer surface uses `--fs-modal-surface` (96% opaque), 12px backdrop blur only over a photo context, a subtle left border and 16–20px padding. The header contains `Notifications`, unread count and `Mark all as read`. Use separate filter tabs/segments for `All`, `Alerts`, `Reports`, `Tasks` and `System`; each notification row has a 20px icon, 14–15px title, 13–14px summary, timestamp, farm/field context and action affordance. Avoid tiny body text and avoid wrapping every single row in a heavily bordered card; use dividers and a selected/hover state.

Notification severity (info/caution/high/critical) is distinct from lifecycle status (unread/read/acknowledged/snoozed/dismissed/resolved). A row's action depends on type: `View field`, `Open report`, `Review task`, `Retry run`, `Restore alert` or `Snooze`. Dismissal is not resolution. Critical active alerts may not be silently hidden by a routine “mark read” command. Duplicate suppression uses an alert group key and only re-notifies when the evidence or severity materially changes, or the documented repeat window expires.

**State and routing:** a click marks read as appropriate then routes using the exact `entityRef`/`reportId`/`taskId`, preserving farm/field context. Closing a drawer returns focus to the bell. Empty state uses a calm single-line explanation; failure to load shows Retry; partial results show what is missing. The unread badge count is derived from canonical notifications, not a separate mutable count.

**Backend:** list endpoint paginates/filter by user and access scope; update endpoint accepts notification ID + desired transition, validates transition in domain layer, and returns canonical state. Snooze stores until timestamp; dismiss and resolve are different commands. A row must not be shown to users who do not have access to its linked farm. Any API failure keeps the current row state or rolls back an optimistic mark-read action and shows a toast.


Group notifications into actionable alerts, report completion/failure, schedule health, upcoming task and system/data notices. Each notification has a stable ID, type, created time, status, related farm/field, deep link and short plain-language description. Acknowledge/read/dismiss/snooze are distinct actions; dismissing an alert does not resolve the underlying condition. Define retention and prevent repeated copies of the same alert unless severity or evidence materially changes.

When clicked, route to the exact report, evidence, task or field and preserve its origin. Notification detail in Advanced Mode may show observed/reference value, anomaly, dataset and timestamp. Simple Mode starts with human-readable wording and a direct next step. If an alert has insufficient confidence, use “Check this” / “Possible issue” rather than definitive wording.

### 30.13 Scenario Simulator

#### Quantified simulator canvas and model contract

The simulator uses a 12-column desktop grid: controls/workspace setup in columns 1–4 (33.3%); baseline-vs-scenario visual comparison in columns 5–9 (41.7%); impact summary and diagnostics in columns 10–12 (25%). At 1024px, use two columns with controls at 35–40% and results 60–65%; below 768px stack controls above results. Keep a sticky compact context bar showing farm, field, baseline rotation/report, scenario name and `Scenario only`. Use an image/3D scene only if it represents a clearly labelled conceptual model; do not render fake precision field geometry or pretend illustrative 3D crop blocks are simulated satellite results.

Controls are grouped by dimension: weather (temperature/rainfall only within supported bounds), water availability/irrigation assumptions, soil-property assumptions if the model supports recalculation, and rotation choice. Each slider shows the exact current delta and unit, baseline value, min/max and a one-sentence explanation. `Reset` returns controls to zero deltas. Scenario presets must disclose their assumptions. Run button changes to `Running scenario…` and uses actual calculation state. Baseline/simulation charts use identical axes, units, time windows and series definitions. Impact summary rows explain both magnitude and direction (e.g. water demand up/down); a higher score is not always better if a floor is breached.

**Calculation contract:** scenario is a deep-cloned input snapshot/delta applied to the same deterministic model pipeline, with identical random seed/engine version so differences are attributable to the scenario input rather than randomness. Recompute series from changed variables; do not apply unexplained fixed score multipliers. Use stable rotation IDs. If a rotation becomes infeasible under the changed scenario, state `No longer feasible` with constraint/reason instead of falling back to an unchanged base candidate. Distinguish `rank changed` from `safety floor breached`. If a delta extends beyond the farm's historical range, reduce scenario confidence and flag extrapolation. With all deltas equal to zero, results must reproduce the baseline exactly.

Saved scenarios have create/rename/duplicate/delete states and are separate records (`scenarioOnly=true`). `Apply to real plan` opens a diff panel that shows each changed field, downstream affected results and a confirmation requirement; the commit creates new versioned inputs and marks dependent outputs stale. It must never mutate an old report. Backend calculations use validated DTOs, timeouts and cancellation; identical input hash/version may reuse a cached result. Show job/data failure explicitly rather than rendering fake impact cards.


Header explains “Test a possible change without editing the real farm.” User selects a farm/field and a baseline analysis/rotation. Left/control area includes scenario name, controls for supported variables (e.g. temperature/rainfall shifts or water availability only where modelling supports it), presets, reset-to-baseline and saved scenarios. Right/results area shows baseline vs scenario, rank changes, component delta, risk drivers and a textual explanation.

The simulator must state the scenario time range and assumptions. It must not relabel scenario numbers as forecasts. Unsupported control/variable combinations should be disabled with a reason. Results must be deterministic for identical inputs and model versions. Save/duplicate/rename/delete scenario actions all work. “Apply to real plan” opens a review diff of changed assumptions and requires explicit confirmation, then creates a new version; it never mutates historical scenarios or reports.

### 30.14 Calendar & Actions

#### Quantified calendar geometry, event rendering and mutation contract

At 1440px the calendar page uses a 12-column grid: calendar body spans 8 columns, selected-date agenda/action panel spans 4 columns; the calendar fills available width (not a small boxed widget centred in a wide page). Main content max-width 1480px with 36–40px gutters, page title 30–34px. Calendar surface is solid/strong glass rather than very transparent because small dates need stable contrast; 12px radius and 20px padding. The month header is a three-part grid: previous arrow in fixed 40px left cell, centered month/year in a flexible middle cell, next arrow in fixed 40px right cell. Never align arrows using the text length of the month string. Day headers are 12–13px; day cells use a stable seven-column CSS grid with min-height 84–110px desktop, 56–72px tablet and adaptive mobile agenda view. Crop labels use 12–13px readable text and never overflow into adjacent day cells.

Use event type + border/marker + label to distinguish crop stage, planting/harvest window, irrigation task, scheduled analysis, alert and farmer observation. Crop-specific hues can be assigned from a restrained, colorblind-considered palette, but each item must carry a crop name and farm/field label; color alone cannot distinguish simultaneous events from different farms. Multi-farm day cells show compact stacked chips or a `+N more` disclosure. Selected date gets a clear 2px border/focus ring with a non-color indicator. The right agenda orders records by time, then severity/type, and shows event title, farm/field, time window, date basis (`planned`, `observed`, `modelled`) and action button.

**Domain model:** calendar event is a view projection from crop stages, tasks, alert due dates, scheduled analysis and user events. It should not duplicate them into separate unsynchronised event records. Task updates use an action command (`complete`, `defer`, `reschedule`, `cancel`) and append an audit event. Completion means the farmer reports the action happened; it does not prove the desired agronomic outcome. Editing planting/harvest dates invalidates dependent rotation timelines, expected crop stage, water/crop demand calculations and relevant Deep Analysis summaries. Record planned vs actual dates separately.

Clicking a crop opens the exact farm + field + active plan + crop stage using canonical IDs, not name-only search. When multiple farms grow the same crop, a click on a specific event uses that event's entity IDs; a generic crop label opens a chooser. ICS export includes timezone, stable UID, sequence/version and cancellation update where supported. Empty month, unavailable date data and failed calendar load get distinct states. At mobile, default to Agenda view; month-grid remains accessible with horizontal/vertical support without whole-page overflow.


The page combines time-based crop stages and actionable farm work while retaining their different data types. Header: title, farm scope, field filter, current month/week/agenda toggle, Today, date navigation, create task and filters. Calendar grid must use screen width efficiently and not be trapped in a small box with unused margins.

Month grid has fixed-position previous/next controls; centered month/year; accessible day headers; clear crop labels within relevant cells; crop-color palette with readable contrast; visually different but legible marks when multiple farms have events on the same day; selected day state; and a textual agenda panel. Do not rely on background color alone to distinguish overlapping farm events.

Selected-date panel lists events chronologically: crop stage/planting/harvest window, recommended task, scheduled Deep Analysis, farmer record and any alert. Each row displays the event type, farm/field, date basis (planned/observed/modelled), status and actions. Clicking an event opens detail; clicking a crop uses the canonical deep link to the exact rotation stage; completing a task updates the action record and report context, not its underlying evidence. Dates derived from a model are windows, not exact promises.

Support create/edit/reschedule/complete/defer/cancel for tasks with audit history. Conflicts with the crop plan should trigger a warning. Changing planting/harvest dates marks affected rotation timelines and analyses stale. `Today` returns to the actual local date for the farm or selected timezone and does not reset unrelated filters.

### 30.15 Data & Trust Centre

#### Quantified data table, provenance drawer and source-state contract

The Data & Trust Centre is a technical inspection workspace. Use a compact top summary bar for `Connected`, `Degraded`, `Unavailable` and `Planned` source counts only from real connector registry records. Below, provide a searchable/filterable dataset table occupying the full content width. Table columns: source/product; variable; connector status; native spatial resolution; temporal cadence/coverage; last observation/period; last retrieval; quality state; official source/docs. Use real table semantics, sticky header where helpful, 13–14px table text and sufficient row height; never turn each dataset into a giant card. Use 86% opaque surfaces or solid background for tables. Status tags use icon + text; never label a planned connector `Online`.

Selecting a dataset or value opens a right-side Evidence Drawer (420–520px desktop, full-height sheet mobile). The drawer is grouped into: `Value`, `Where/when`, `Source`, `Quality`, `Transformation`, `Formula/model`, `Confidence/limitations` and `References`. Each value includes its original/source type, normalised value/unit, observation period and retrieval timestamp; grid/pixel size and field area comparison; valid-pixel/coverage details if applicable; QC flags; fallback chain; actual transformation; engine/dataset version; uncertainty basis; and why the value is used. Calculation Explorer shows readable formula, each substituted input and units, intermediate terms, output and version. Use a monospaced style only for formula/IDs, not body prose.

**Backend/source registry contract:** source status is computed from connector health and latest source outcomes, with timestamp and error class. A “Test connection” action executes a real bounded health check and returns its HTTP/service result; do not simulate success with `setTimeout`. API keys stay server-side. Dataset entries shown in the UI must be generated from sources actually configured and must distinguish `Integrated`, `Planned`, `Unavailable`, `Degraded` and `Demo`. An analysis report references the exact provenance record used at run time; the Data Centre cannot retroactively rewrite past source state.

If a source fails, show whether the variable was replaced by an allowed model/reanalysis/prior, is missing, or blocks a calculation. Fallback is per variable and explicitly tagged; never blend demo values into live results silently. If a value isn't available, say so and name the missing dependency. WCAG focus and keyboard access apply to table row, drawer tabs and copy/export actions; opening the drawer must not move the map or selected farm context.


Page purpose: “Show me what Field Shift knows, where it came from, and how it reached a conclusion.”

Top overview: active/inactive/degraded connectors and last successful retrieval for the selected farm/time window. Dataset table fields: product/source, variable, actual connection status (Integrated / Planned / Unavailable / Degraded), native resolution, time coverage/cadence, last retrieved/observation date, quality notes, license/attribution and official docs link.

A value-level Evidence Drawer contains actual raw/normalised value, units and standardisation; source/type; source timestamp and retrieval time; spatial footprint vs field size; quality flags; fallback chain; transformation and formula; model version; confidence basis and limitations. A calculation explorer shows formula in readable form, each input substituted with actual values/units, outputs/intermediate values, assumptions and references. It does not claim a named formula unless the implementation is actually that formula.

If a source connector is only planned, label it **Planned** and do not populate live status. Do not claim NASA, SMAP or Sentinel-2 data were fetched merely because a banner says NASA. The registry itself must be generated from the connectors and provenance records actually used in each run, not from a static list that overstates the product.

### 30.16 Settings

#### Quantified settings forms and persistence contract

Settings is a grouped preferences workspace, not one long form. On desktop use a left navigation rail of 220–250px and right settings content pane; at 768px collapse the rail to a select/segmented control, and at 430px stack each group. The page header is 30–34px. Use grouped form sections with a small title/description and rows separated by 1px neutral dividers. Do not create a separate card for every toggle. Standard settings surface is solid `--fs-surface-solid`; modal confirmation uses `--fs-modal-surface` (96% opacity), 20px radius and 24px padding. Label text 14–15px, helper 13–14px. Toggle/input height at least 44px with visible focus states.

Groups: display/mode/units/timezone; default farm and scope; automation schedule; notifications; local vs remote data/privacy; integrations/source status; export/deletion; about, versions and methodology. Each setting describes the scope: `This device`, `This workspace`, `This farm`, or `Scheduled analysis service`. Do not present a preference as account-wide when it is stored only in localStorage. Remove billing unless real billing exists. Avoid settings values that require an unsupported backend; disable with a clear reason or omit until implementation is available.

**Save semantics:** simple local preferences may save immediately with a visible `Saved` indicator; multi-field settings use `Save changes` and show `Unsaved changes` with Cancel/Discard. Automation/data-sharing changes require review of their consequences and consent flow. If save fails, retain edit values, show a retryable error and do not switch the visual state to saved. A change to timezone triggers recomputation of displayed local timestamps but does not rewrite absolute UTC job/report timestamps. Unit changes alter formatting at render time; canonical stored values remain metric/canonical to prevent calculation differences.

Privacy actions clearly differentiate `Clear this device's local farm data`, `Delete remote scheduled-analysis snapshot`, `Disable schedule`, `Delete reports` and `Export data`. Each destructive action names the records affected and whether recovery is possible; require confirmation and re-authentication if remote account operations require it. Settings page and global Simple/Advanced mode use the same canonical preference store. Keep the mode toggle at the top of the Display group and ensure toggling modes does not clear page filters, selected farm or current task context.


Settings is divided into sections, not a long flat form:

- **Display:** Simple/Advanced mode toggle; light/dark mode only if supported; text/units and timezone; motion preference.
- **Farm defaults:** default farm, default units, inherited practice settings with scope clearly stated.
- **Automation:** enabled schedules, cadence, timezone, last run status, consent/data snapshot, change/pause/disable.
- **Notifications:** alert types, report-ready, failures, daily/weekly summary and quiet hours.
- **Data and privacy:** what stays local, what syncs, scheduled snapshot details, export, clear local data, revoke cloud schedule consent, cloud report deletion and retention explanation.
- **Connections:** current source status and any account/permission needs; no fake connection status.
- **About and methodology:** engine version, crop knowledge base version, sources and limitations.

Remove billing from Settings unless a real implemented billing system is explicitly required later. All settings copy should use clear plain language. Every save has visible state; changes that affect an active schedule are explained before commit.

## 31. Canonical data model and event contracts

The prior data model must be expanded sufficiently for implementation. Use typed schemas and stable IDs. Exact database technology may vary; semantic contracts may not.

### 31.1 Core entities

- **User/LocalWorkspace:** workspace ID, owner/auth subject if applicable, created date, locale/timezone, preferences, data mode and consent records. Anonymous local-only use must remain possible if chosen; scheduled remote processing requires a secure identity/signed-device binding.
- **Farm:** ID, display name, region/country, timezone, default units, optional farm-level polygon, centroid, notes, created/updated timestamps, archived/deleted state and version.
- **Field:** ID, farm ID, display name, geometry version, area and method, centroid, soil profile references, current crop/rotation ID, profile completeness and timestamps.
- **GeometryVersion:** ID, farm/field ID, geometry, CRS, area/perimeter, source/import method, validation results, effective timestamp, created timestamp and superseded version ID.
- **FarmPractice / FieldPractice:** practice type, structured value/unit, schedule/frequency, effective date range, scope, provenance, original user text if supplied, parsed output, ambiguity flags, user confirmation timestamp and stale/unknown status.
- **CropHistoryEvent:** field ID, crop ID/family, start/end or season, dates certainty (`exact`, `estimated`, `unknown`), source, disease/pest notes and user-confirmed yields if provided.
- **CropKnowledgeVersion:** crop ID, family, duration bounds, crop coefficients/thresholds/source references, planting/harvest conditions, water/nutrient traits, host-family constraints, evidence quality and version. No agronomic constant without a source/reasoning record.
- **AnalysisInputSnapshot:** stable snapshot ID and hash; farm/field/geometry/profile/practice/history version IDs; source payload references and time windows; user priorities; engine/crop database versions; consent state; created timestamp.
- **DataObservation:** source/product/variable, raw and normalised value, unit, start/end timestamp, retrieval timestamp, geometry/pixel/grid information, QC flags, source type (observation/model/reanalysis/estimate), cache ID and provenance ID.
- **DerivedMetric:** metric key, value/unit, time window, calculation ID/version, input references, uncertainty/confidence basis, availability state and explanation keys.
- **RotationPlan:** ID, farm/field, created date, plan status, crop sequence/stages, active version, priority weights, source snapshot, total duration, review triggers and user decision state.
- **RotationCandidate:** analysis ID, candidate ID, sequence, season schedule, hard-constraint checks, total match and component scores, risks/trade-offs, confidence and stable rank explanation.
- **AnalysisRun:** run ID/type/scope, trigger (`manual`, `scheduled`, `recompute`, `scenario`), status, input snapshot ID, source outcomes, stage events, retry count, start/end timestamps, engine versions, result/report ID and failure classification.
- **DeepAnalysisReport:** immutable report ID/version, run ID, farm summary, per-field results, findings, actions, data gaps, previous-report comparison references and export metadata.
- **Alert:** ID, affected entity, type/severity/status, condition/threshold version, evidence references, first/last seen, suppression group, acknowledgment/resolution history and destination route.
- **Action/Task:** ID, affected farm/field/plan/finding, title and description, rationale, source references, status, due window, created-by, completion evidence/notes, deferral reason and revision history.
- **ObservationJournalEntry:** ID, farm/field, event type, narrative, normalized values/units, original user text if applicable, attachments, date/location confidence, author and privacy state.
- **Scenario:** ID, baseline run/plan version, modified input deltas, units, assumptions, name, creation date, result, status and `scenarioOnly=true` marker.
- **Schedule:** ID, owner/workspace, scope, cadence, timezone, next run, enabled state, consent/version, permitted snapshot reference, last/next run, failures, retry policy and delivery preferences.
- **ReportComparison:** IDs of two immutable report versions, scope compatibility, metric deltas, driver categories and comparability status.
- **AuditEvent:** event key, entity reference, user/system actor, timestamp, success/failure, safe metadata and correlation/job ID; never raw secrets.

### 31.2 Entity relationships and invariants

- One farm may contain many fields; a field belongs to one farm at a time, with any relocation represented as a versioned action rather than implicit reassignment.
- A rotation plan belongs to a field; if a farm-level plan spans several fields, each field's sequence and constraints are explicit child plan records.
- One analysis run references exactly one immutable input snapshot.
- One final report references one run and its source/model versions.
- A scheduled run records the exact snapshot/version it used; later local edits do not retroactively alter the report.
- A task may reference a finding but remains a separate record: closing a task does not automatically close an alert or prove improvement.
- Geometry edits create versions; historical analyses reference the geometry version used at calculation time.
- Scenario inputs are isolated from real farm/profile records until a user explicitly applies a reviewed change.
- Source observations are immutable source evidence; normalized/derived values are separate records with transformation/version links.
- Deleted/archived items must not remain selectable as active, but their retention/export behaviour follows the stated deletion policy.

### 31.3 Explicit status types, not overloaded strings

Use enums/unions for status and availability. For example:

- Data availability: `available`, `partial`, `stale`, `unavailable`, `not_applicable`, `demo_only`.
- Evidence type: `observed`, `satellite_product`, `modelled`, `reanalysis`, `farmer_reported`, `farmer_confirmed`, `regional_prior`, `derived`, `scenario_assumption`, `demo`.
- Analysis status: `draft`, `queued`, `validating`, `fetching_data`, `quality_control`, `calculating`, `generating_explanations`, `complete`, `partial`, `failed`, `cancelled`, `retrying`.
- Plan status: `draft`, `candidate`, `active`, `review_recommended`, `superseded`, `archived`.
- Alert status: `new`, `acknowledged`, `snoozed`, `resolved`, `dismissed`, `reopened`.
- Task status: `suggested`, `planned`, `in_progress`, `completed`, `deferred`, `dismissed`, `cancelled`.

Do not encode multiple states into free-form labels and then guess status from text in the UI.

## 32. Backend architecture, APIs and scheduled jobs

### 32A. API contract precision — required for each backend operation

Every remote action must have a written operation contract containing: HTTP method/path; authentication/authorization requirements; request DTO schema; response DTO schema; validation rules; side effects; idempotency policy; timeout; retryability; cancellation semantics; persistence effects; log/correlation fields; and error codes. Example required error envelope:

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE",
    "message": "Historical rainfall data could not be retrieved.",
    "retryable": true,
    "correlationId": "opaque-request-id",
    "details": { "sourceId": "NASA_POWER", "stage": "fetch_precipitation" }
  }
}
```

Do not expose stack traces, keys, private prompt contents or exact farm coordinates in user-visible errors. Use appropriate HTTP status semantics (400 schema/validation issue, 401 unauthenticated, 403 not authorised, 404 unknown/inaccessible record, 409 stale-version or conflicting state, 413 upload too large, 429 rate limited, 5xx server/source failure). Validate both browser inputs and server-side payloads; client validation is UX, not a security boundary.

For long-running jobs, submit a job and return a stable job ID plus status; client may subscribe via Server-Sent Events (SSE) or poll a documented status endpoint. Progress events include `jobId`, `stageKey`, safe display label, stage state, timestamp, optional `sourceId` and safe error code. A percentage is optional unless stage weights are defensible. Do not manufacture percentage progress. A cancellation request is best-effort and records whether the calculation stopped or the result was merely ignored at the client.

Every write has optimistic concurrency/version policy. For edits, include `expectedVersion` or an ETag; conflicting writes return 409 and prompt reload/merge rather than overwriting a newer change. For schedules and report generation, use idempotency keys to prevent duplicate jobs on retry. Keep local-only CRUD paths working if the remote scheduler is unavailable; do not silently route private full farm records to remote storage.

### 32B. Backend observability and connector adapter contract

All provider calls go through typed connector adapters rather than being issued ad hoc from page components. Each adapter normalises payloads into a canonical observation schema while preserving raw-product references, timestamps, units, QA flags and provenance. Configure connection/read timeouts, bounded concurrency, retry with exponential backoff/jitter for transient failures, and rate limits from provider docs. Do not retry permanent 4xx/authentication/invalid-query errors blindly. Cache keys include native spatial cell/grid, variable/product, time window, product/version and relevant processing options; do not share private per-farm content under an overly coarse public cache key.

Structured logs include severity, timestamp, service, operation, correlation ID, run ID and safe error class. Logs must not include access tokens, API keys, user secrets, full private prompts, or precise location data unless specifically justified and protected. Add metrics for connector latency, error rate, cache hit ratio, job queue depth, job completion duration, retry rate and schedule lateness. Provider health displayed to users is derived from these actual checks, not a visual-only API ping. Ensure logs/metrics never become a shadow store of sensitive agronomic profiles.


### 32.1 Local-first with explicit scheduled-analysis bridge

Default farm data lives locally where practical (IndexedDB preferred for structured records and larger local data; localStorage only for compact preferences/selected IDs). The small backend exists for scheduled jobs and any explicitly authorised cloud operations. Because a closed browser cannot be read by a server, the product must define the secure bridge:

1. user enables a schedule;
2. frontend presents the exact minimum dataset required by that schedule;
3. user explicitly consents to transmitting that snapshot;
4. snapshot is encrypted in transit and at rest under access control;
5. backend scheduler operates only on that approved snapshot plus permitted refreshed public source data;
6. each job stores input snapshot version/hash, source and result metadata;
7. results are retrievable only by the workspace owner/authorised client;
8. revoking consent prevents future execution, deletes queued data where possible and follows the disclosed retention policy for past reports;
9. sync conflict rules are explicit if local practices change while an older schedule snapshot is pending.

If the user declines cloud snapshots, local analysis can still be run when the app is open; do not falsely advertise unattended scheduling. Explain the choice with simple language.

### 32.2 Service separation

Keep clear interfaces among:

- UI/presentation state;
- domain/application service layer;
- typed API adapters;
- analysis orchestrator/job runner;
- individual external data connectors;
- data cleaning/QC and provenance registry;
- crop knowledge base;
- deterministic calculation engine;
- explanation/claim-validation layer;
- job scheduler and notification delivery;
- local persistence and optional cloud persistence;
- reporting/export service.

The scientific engine should be a deterministic, independently testable module with no direct network or UI dependencies. AI services should not directly set scientific scores or invent source observations.

### 32.3 Minimum API contract families

Implement equivalent endpoints/actions (exact REST names can differ) with typed validation, auth/authorization, request IDs and consistent errors:

**Workspace/profile:** get/update local or account profile, list/create/update/archive farms, list/create/update/archive fields, geometry validate/save/import/export, list versions.

**Wizard:** create draft, autosave answer, get/resume draft, validate current answer, prefill optional evidence suggestions, retrieve review model, update inline answer, start analysis.

**Analysis:** create run, retrieve run, subscribe to progress (SSE/WebSocket/poll fallback), cancel eligible run, retrieve immutable results, retrieve stage/source status, rerun against current inputs.

**Rotations:** list candidates, get candidate details, compare two/three candidates, set active plan, create plan version, mark review recommended, export plan.

**Deep Analysis:** run analysis for selected scope, list/retrieve reports, compare reports, retrieve findings, list/create/complete/defer actions, attach note/observation, request report export.

**Schedule:** create schedule, update cadence/timezone, enable after consent, pause/resume/disable, run now, retrieve job history, revoke snapshot consent.

**Simulator:** run scenario with validated baseline ID and supported deltas, save/rename/duplicate/delete scenario, compare scenarios, request apply-to-real-plan review.

**Sources:** list source registry, inspect data coverage/source status for a region/time, retrieve provenance for a metric, inspect methodology version.

All list endpoints need pagination and documented filtering. Validate all ownership relationships on the server. A valid `fieldId` alone does not prove that it belongs to the requested `farmId`.

### 32.4 Job payload and progress events

A job payload includes: run ID, workspace/owner ID, scope IDs, trigger, immutable input snapshot ID/hash, source requests, expected stage names, engine and crop KB versions, timeout/retry policy and idempotency key. Never include raw credentials in a payload visible to the client.

Progress events include `runId`, `stageKey`, human-readable stage label, status (`pending`, `running`, `complete`, `skipped`, `partial`, `failed`), start/end times, safe message and completion ratio only where measurable. Stage messages must not leak stack traces or secrets. Percent should reflect actual completed work/weighted stages, not a timer pretending to be progress.

Use exponential backoff with jitter for transient failures, explicit timeouts, limited retries and circuit-breaking/backoff for persistently failing providers. Distinguish rate limit, timeout, unauthorized, invalid response, no coverage and internal calculation error. A retry must not create duplicate reports or notifications.

### 32.5 Cache strategy and provenance

Cache data by provider/product + native grid cell or geometry lookup + variable/band + start/end time + product/version + processing options. Use source-appropriate TTLs: static historical datasets may be cached long-term; forecasts and recent conditions expire quickly; soil priors persist with product version and retrieval date. Never reuse a cached value from a different geometry/time window without recording how it was aggregated. Cache invalidation must be targeted after geometry, crop, practice or source-version changes.

### 32.6 Failure, partial success and retry contract

- If one nonessential source fails, continue with valid sources and label the result Partial if the missing source materially affects coverage.
- If a required input/source for a specific calculation is missing, mark that metric unavailable and disable derived actions requiring it.
- If all critical data inputs fail validation, fail the run rather than produce a confident report from defaults.
- Never use a demo value as a silent fallback in a real farm run.
- A rerun creates a new run and report; retrying one failed job retains its identity and attempt history.
- Show what failed, what still succeeded, how it affects the conclusion, and whether retry is useful.

## 33. Scientific logic, confidence and score governance

### 33A. Scientific metric contract and naming grammar

Every number in the UI, report, map inspector, comparison, notification, downloadable brief and AI explanation must have a metric registry entry before it ships. Required fields: stable `metricKey`; display label; definition; unit; spatial and temporal basis; observation/model/calculation type; source/product; input fields; formula/method version; source reference; valid range; missing-data policy; uncertainty/confidence method; allowed page surfaces; formatting precision; status thresholds and who approved the threshold. The UI renderer should obtain labels, units, availability, provenance and status from this registry instead of independently hardcoding them in each component.

Name metrics unambiguously: `precipitation_observed_mm_24h`, `precipitation_reanalysis_mm_14d`, `soil_moisture_percentile_grid`, `root_zone_wetness_modelled`, `vegetation_index_ndvi_composite`, `crop_water_demand_estimated_mm_day`, `rotation_match_score` and `evidence_confidence`. Do not collapse concepts with different semantics into a generic `water`, `soil`, `health` or `score` property. UI copy may be concise but the schema and evidence drawer use canonical keys.

Any calculated score must disclose: numerator/denominator or component structure; scaling range; weights; hard constraints/caps; baseline; missing component treatment; rounding; version; and tests. A visual dial/radial gauge is only a visual representation of an already-computed value; it must not own or invent the value. For uncertainty intervals, label the interval method (e.g. P10–P90) and do not use an arbitrary shaded band.

### 33B. Exact client/backend responsibility boundary

Client: interaction state, local draft state, formatting/unit display, view-mode toggling, derived display grouping and safe offline queueing. Backend/domain service: authorization, schema validation, durable scheduling, canonical source/analysis jobs, server-side keys, immutable reports and cross-device state if accounts are enabled. Shared pure domain modules: formulas/rotation scoring where possible, tested on both client/server using the same inputs/version. The browser must not be the authority for permissions, score thresholds, job state or successful remote persistence. A render component must not independently reimplement agronomic rules.

### 33C. Model/version comparability

An `AnalysisResult` references engineVersion, cropKnowledgeVersion, inputSnapshotId, source snapshot IDs and calculation settings. If a calculation changes, previous reports remain immutable and new comparisons carry a compatibility flag. A direct numeric delta is allowed only when units, scopes, time windows, methods and data semantics are comparable. If not, show a quality/coverage/method change explanation rather than a single misleading delta.


### 33.1 Metric catalogue required before implementing a number

For every displayed score/metric the builder must register:

- stable metric key and human-readable name;
- exact meaning and decision question;
- mathematical formula or reference implementation;
- required inputs, units, temporal range and geometry basis;
- output range/unit and directionality (higher better, lower better, or contextual);
- source evidence type and fallback rules;
- quality filters and missing-data policy;
- model/calculation version;
- validated threshold/interpretation ranges;
- confidence method and known limitations;
- pages/components allowed to display it;
- text used in Simple and Advanced modes;
- tests and evidence/reference citations.

Do not render metrics that do not have this definition. If a metric is planned but not computable, display it in a clearly marked planned/unavailable context rather than inventing a plausible-looking number.

### 33.2 Crop rotation decision engine

Separate hard constraints from weighted preferences:

**Hard constraints** reject sequences that violate supported planting windows, crop availability, minimum family return interval, maximum repeated host/crop-family exposure, crop duration constraints, infeasible transition dates, known water constraints or explicitly user-required/excluded crops. Missing optional values should usually reduce confidence rather than act as an invented hard failure, unless there is a clear agronomic/safety constraint.

**Candidate generation:** produce a diverse set of valid sequences; avoid top N near-duplicates; include meaningful differences in order, family diversity, duration or user-defined constraints. Use deterministic search (e.g. beam search/DP when suitable) with an input-hash seed for tie-breaking. Identical normalized inputs + model versions must produce equivalent ranked outputs.

**Candidate score:** calculate component metrics separately; normalize each with a documented scale; normalize user weights; clamp weights only when defined by product policy and disclose conflicts; calculate the composite from supported components; retain intermediate values for explanation. Do not make component values appear empirical when they are model-based. A score difference of 1 point should not be described as a meaningful winner unless the method supports such a claim.

**Duration and cycle length:** evaluate crop durations allowed by the crop knowledge base; penalize unsupported assumptions about perennial crops, declining performance, disease pressure and feasible planting windows. Evaluate total cycle length; disclose what a “season” means for the location/cropping system. Show review timing based on relevant slow-changing soil factors, data confidence and risk triggers. Do not assume every rotation is four seasons or years.

**Soil dynamics:** only quantify nitrogen, organic matter, carbon or soil-health changes if input/transition models and crop traits are sourced and validated sufficiently. Track units/basis and distinguish a modeled projection from measured soil change. Do not promise microbial activity, disease reduction percentages or nitrogen credits without a transparent evidence basis.

**Fallback/output behaviour:** if no sequence passes hard constraints, show no acceptable rotation under current assumptions and list realistic inputs/constraints that could be reconsidered. Do not manufacture a recommended crop. Always present downsides and mitigations alongside benefits.

### 33.3 Water intelligence

Distinguish reference evapotranspiration (ET₀), crop evapotranspiration (ETc), effective rainfall, reported/observed irrigation and estimated root-zone water state. If implementing FAO-56 Penman–Monteith, use the actual required inputs and unit conventions; if using a simpler proxy, label it a proxy and disclose the approximation—never label a temperature-only heuristic as FAO-56. Document crop-coefficient (`Kc`) and growth-stage assumptions and their source. Show the water-balance period, starting condition, soil water capacity and what is unknown. Do not present rainfall total as identical to effective rainfall.

### 33.4 Soil and vegetation intelligence

Mapped global soil properties are regional/model estimates with native grid resolution and uncertainty; they are not a laboratory test from the user's exact field. The UI must show property, depth interval, units, source, retrieval/version, prediction uncertainty when available and any gap between field size and map resolution.

Vegetation indicators require actual source imagery/index calculations and product quality controls such as cloud/no-data masks and valid scene dates. NDVI cannot be derived from rainfall. Vegetation trend cannot by itself prove disease, nutrient deficiency or irrigation failure. Where no valid optical data exists, show unavailable/cloud-obscured and retain other evidence.

### 33.5 Confidence methodology

Confidence is not a decorative percentage. The team must define confidence levels or a calibrated score from measurable factors such as input completeness, source quality, temporal freshness, spatial fit, cross-source consistency and model applicability. Publish the factors and validation strategy. Until calibrated, use evidence labels such as High / Moderate / Limited with an explicit qualitative basis; do not invent a false-precision confidence number.

Missing-data behaviour must be metric-specific. Missing values are not zero. Never interpolate beyond declared limits. If a fallback source replaces a preferred source, preserve both the attempted primary source status and the actual fallback provenance, and lower confidence if appropriate.

### 33.6 Farm-wide score governance

A farm-wide score is not a required single number until the methodology is defensible. Initially show a farm-wide condition summary plus each field's score/indicators and the worst significant field alerts. If/when a composite index is approved:

- publish components and weights;
- define aggregation by area and whether each component is comparable across fields;
- prevent missing fields silently inflating/deflating the score;
- show coverage and excluded/missing fields;
- retain component and per-field metrics alongside the composite;
- version the score formula and prevent naïve comparisons across incompatible versions.

## 34. External product research: inspiration to adapt, not copy

Research was performed on publicly available official pages/documentation for farm-management and precision-agriculture products. Borrow interaction patterns and product lessons, not proprietary branding, page screenshots, copy or unique visual compositions.

### 34.1 OneSoil — field creation and boundary editing

OneSoil documents multiple ways to create fields: draw boundaries, select detected fields where supported, and import field files. It supports vertex edits and saving boundaries; some workflows also use satellite imagery/index layers to refine the boundary. Inspiration for Field Shift: offer a flexible field-creation entry point and a clear review/save cycle, make geometry editing discoverable, and let the farmer improve a boundary instead of treating the first outline as permanent. Adaptation: Field Shift must make source/coverage limitations visible and should not promise automatic detections where no provider exists.

Source: [OneSoil — How to add fields](https://help.onesoil.ai/en/articles/5235093-how-to-add-fields-in-the-onesoil-platform); [OneSoil — edit field boundaries](https://help.onesoil.ai/en/articles/5237448-how-to-edit-field-information-and-boundaries).

### 34.2 farmOS — locations, event records and tasks

farmOS connects location assets, logs/events, plans, observations and tasks. Its guide shows that a map can be a navigation entry point into connected records, and that planned tasks can later be marked completed, rescheduled or deleted. Inspiration: Field Shift should use relationships between fields, activities, observations and reports instead of treating each page as isolated data. Adaptation: keep Field Shift's simpler farmer-first UI and specialised evidence/rotation engine; do not force the user to manage a complex raw record database for everyday tasks.

Sources: [farmOS User Guide](https://farmos.org/guide/); [farmOS Mapping locations](https://farmos.org/guide/mapping/); [farmOS Logging events](https://farmos.org/guide/logs/).

### 34.3 John Deere Operations Center — operational continuity

John Deere positions Operations Center around setting up farm operations, planning work, monitoring job quality/productivity and learning from seasonal results; it includes farm maps and field-operation context. Inspiration: connect the recommendation to execution and later review, and make operational status accessible on web/tablet/phone. Adaptation: avoid claims of equipment integrations or near-real-time machine monitoring unless Field Shift actually implements those integrations.

Source: [John Deere Operations Center](https://www.deere.com/en-us/products-solutions/technology-solutions/precision-ag-technology/operations-center).

### 34.4 Climate FieldView — comparative field analysis and reports

Climate FieldView describes field-level charts, side-by-side maps, data visualization and custom reports. Inspiration: let Field Shift users compare two rotations, two dated reports or two relevant field layers side by side, and export a clean field/farm brief. Adaptation: comparative visuals must preserve source dates, scale differences and confidence; comparison is not valid when units or datasets are incompatible.

Source: [Climate FieldView — Field Health Imagery](https://climate.com/features/field-health-imagery/).

### 34.5 EOSDA Crop Monitoring — exclusion areas and image selection

EOSDA's user guide documents boundary tools, cutting out parts of a field and choosing available imagery by date. Inspiration: support exclusion zones where needed, explain net cultivable area, and allow selection among actually available valid imagery dates. Adaptation: never assume a current high-resolution image is available for every location/date, and disclose sensor resolution/cloud coverage.

Source: [EOSDA Crop Monitoring — Add Field](https://eos.com/user-guide/crop-monitoring/add-field/).

### 34.6 What Field Shift should uniquely contribute

Competitor inspiration is a foundation, not the product's differentiation. Field Shift should be defined by the connected reasoning loop: NASA/soil/climate evidence with explicit quality and resolution; deterministic crop-rotation planning and duration logic; per-field Deep Analysis; farmer practice inputs; transparent “why this changed” reports; scheduled reassessments with consent; and recommendations that become trackable actions without pretending that the software controls the farm.

## 35. Complete interaction acceptance matrix

### 35A. Precision design and interaction verification extension

For each matrix row, add four separate acceptance assertions: (1) **visual state** — exact active/disabled/loading/selected/error treatment and layout region; (2) **domain transition** — the state-machine transition and entity mutation; (3) **data effect** — dependency invalidations, saved version, report/schedule/alert changes; (4) **navigation/context** — route destination and preserved farm/field/plan/date context. Passing only one category is not a pass.

For every key page, store approved screenshot baselines at 1440px, 1280px, 768px and 390px. Use visual regression diff thresholds that flag material spacing, wrapping, clipping and contrast changes without failing on harmless antialiasing. Verify computed CSS for design tokens in smoke checks (e.g. expected card alpha, 12px radius, focus outline) where feasible. Test both a dark quiet background and a high-detail photo backdrop to ensure glassmorphic text remains readable.

For each interactive element test keyboard focus/activation, touch-sized target, disabled explanation, duplicate click suppression, save pending/failure handling and accessible status announcements. For each metric test formatting, units, unavailable state, source drawer, stale state and consistent value across Home, Farm Detail, Deep Analysis and Export. Include a “one field critical, other fields healthy” regression test to ensure portfolio aggregation does not mask the critical field.


The builder must convert this matrix into automated tests where possible and manual checks where map/canvas accessibility or provider behaviour requires them.

| Trigger | Expected UI reaction | Data/logic reaction | Failure/edge rule |
|---|---|---|---|
| Change active farm | All farm-context indicators update together; heading/breadcrumb changes | Re-query only the selected farm, cancel stale client requests | Never flash old farm data as current; invalidate field selection if unrelated |
| Change active field | Field-specific cards, rotation, calendar and reports update | Resolve dependencies from field ID and version | Verify field belongs to active farm |
| Click crop tile anywhere | Opens the correct rotation stage and selected field | Resolve by stable IDs | Same crop name in two farms must never cross-link |
| Click farm name/“open details” | Opens that farm detail overview | Route includes farm ID | Never return only to `/farms` generic listing |
| Draw field polygon | Vertices/edge preview/area update live | Store draft geometry until saved | Invalid polygons cannot be saved; draft can be cancelled or undone |
| Move polygon vertex | Selected vertex and shape update; area recomputes | Geometry draft becomes dirty | Large or invalid changes show geometry validation before commit |
| Save boundary | Confirmation and saved status; updated area appears everywhere | New geometry version; invalidate affected spatial analysis | Past reports preserve old boundary version |
| Cancel map edits | Restore persisted geometry | Discard draft with user confirmation if dirty | Never drop unsaved edits silently |
| Switch map layer | Legend, date, provenance and raster change consistently | Fetch/resolve the actual layer for same bounds | Keep field selection; no-data layer explains why |
| Tile/geocoder failure | Error banner + manual alternative | Record provider failure safely | App remains usable; no infinite loader |
| Change crop history | Affected plan/results marked stale | Invalidate family-return/sequence calculations | Unrelated farm/field results remain valid |
| Change irrigation routine | Show save confirmation and affect appropriate outputs | Update practice version; scheduled snapshot policy applies | Does not retroactively alter old reports |
| Save wizard answer | Visible Saved state | Update draft and affected validation state | Save failure remains visible with retry |
| Edit review answer | Inline edit/save/cancel; changed row updated | Mark dependent run stale; recompute when requested | Do not re-run silently on every edit |
| Start rotation plan | Truthful staged progress | Fetch/validate inputs; create run snapshot | Only say a stage completed when actual work completed |
| Source partially fails | Partial-result banner and per-metric status | Continue only independent calculations | No silent demo/default substitutions |
| Select rotation candidate | Main sequence, risks, scores, detail panels all switch together | Use candidate ID and matching snapshot | Do not leave component scores from previous candidate |
| Compare two rotations | Side-by-side results and conclusion update | Compare same metric definitions and units | Incompatible metrics marked not comparable |
| Save as active plan | Confirmation and active label across relevant pages | New plan version and status transition | No historical report rewrite; audit plan selection |
| Change scenario control | Result delta updates with source/assumption label | Recompute scenario deterministically | Does not write to real farm inputs |
| Apply scenario | Review diff modal; user confirms | Create new real input/plan version if accepted | Scenario remains preserved as scenario history |
| Run Deep Analysis | Preflight and stages shown; report added on completion | Freeze snapshot, evaluate fields, aggregate, persist report | Partial/failed run never looks fully successful |
| Enable schedule | Consent and preview completed | Persist schedule and exact permitted snapshot | No scheduled cloud execution without opt-in |
| Rainfall arrives | Relevant future run can incorporate correctly typed data | Update environmental observation/cache and stale dependencies | Forecast is not observation; rain does not imply no irrigation |
| Scheduled job fails | Show retry/status/error reason and last successful report | Retry under limits; record job history | Do not duplicate reports/notifications |
| Complete task | Status updates in calendar/actions | Store completion event and note | Does not automatically resolve alert or claim impact |
| Dismiss alert | Notification is dismissed/suppressed per policy | Underlying signal remains until resolution condition | Explain “dismissed” is not “fixed” |
| Export report | Real generated PDF/CSV is offered | Authorised server/client export uses report snapshot | Failure gives actionable retry; no fake download button |
| Change units | All visible supported values convert consistently | Canonical store remains metric; convert at presentation/input boundary | Never mix units on one table without labels |
| Toggle Advanced mode | Relevant detail is revealed; selection/context persists | Same result model/calculations | No score or recommendation changes due to mode alone |
| Global Undo/Redo | Tooltip identifies action; relevant values restore | Reverse/reapply valid local action and invalidate dependent outputs | External/irreversible effects require compensating action, not false undo |
| Browser Back/Forward | Navigation behaves normally and restores route context | URL state is source of truth for shareable views | Not conflated with domain Undo/Redo |
| Delete farm | Confirmation shows effects/retention | Delete/archive through consistent cascade policy | Must not leave orphaned fields/jobs or misrepresent cloud erasure |
| Keyboard-only operation | All controls and map alternatives accessible | Same operations available | No critical workflow trapped in pointer-only canvas |

## 36. Required automated test suite and release gates

### 36.1 Unit tests

Test: input validation, coordinate ranges, polygon ring closure/self-intersection, geodesic area calculations, unit conversion, score normalization, weights bounds/sum, crop family return intervals, crop duration bounds, cycle length, season/year distinction, sequence hard constraints, nitrogen-credit assumptions where implemented, water-balance unit consistency, confidence/coverage caps, data fallback provenance, determinism and unchanged-input hashing.

### 36.2 Integration tests

Test each source connector's valid response, empty/no-coverage response, HTTP errors, malformed schema, missing fields, rate limits, timeout, retries, cache hit/miss, stale cache, version change, and privacy-safe log output. Test backend access-control checks for cross-user farm/field IDs and cross-farm mismatched IDs. Test schedule creation, consent revocation, idempotent run/retry and report retrieval.

### 36.3 End-to-end workflows

1. First visit → Add Farm → location search or manual coordinate → draw boundary → save → add two fields.
2. Boundary edit → undo/redo vertex → cancel and restore; repeat and save; confirm area and stale analysis updates.
3. Import GeoJSON with multiple polygons, malformed geometry and excluded area; validate outcomes.
4. Run rotation wizard, back/forward through every stage, skip optional answers, close/reopen, inline edit review and re-run.
5. Select candidate 2 and verify all related scores/risks/details update to candidate 2.
6. Compare two rotations; verify conclusion reflects selected weights and disclosed assumptions.
7. Navigate to the same crop from Home, Calendar and rotation page; ensure exact farm/field/rotation/season opens.
8. Run Deep Analysis for all fields; verify report, partial sources, farm summary and each field result.
9. Compare two reports with same engine; then simulate engine version change and verify comparability warning.
10. Enable scheduled analysis with consent, run it, retry a failure, revoke consent and verify future job stops.
11. Confirm forecast rain and observed rainfall are displayed as separate categories; ensure rainfall does not change irrigation record without evidence.
12. Save scenario, test values, compare to baseline, apply through confirmation and verify old reports/scenario remain immutable.
13. Complete a task; ensure calendar updates but alert/field condition does not falsely resolve.
14. Unit switch updates Home, charts, tables, rotation details, field geometry area and exports.
15. Toggle Simple/Advanced; confirm same values and ranks, only detail changes.
16. Keyboard-only path for main wizard, farm creation, rotation selection, report drill-down, calendar and settings.
17. Mobile/desktop tests, browser zoom, reduced motion, screen reader summaries and offline/poor network handling.

### 36.4 Visual release gates

No overlapping text; no clipped crop sequence; no page-wide horizontal overflow at defined test widths; no tiny essential text; consistent typography; clear main action per view; no card nesting clutter; balanced page width; map toolbar/inspector fit; readable legends and tables; focus state visible; animation disabled/reduced appropriately; screenshots reviewed for Home, My Farms, farm detail, map editor, Rotation Results, rotation detail, Deep Analysis, report detail, schedule setup, Simulator, Calendar, Data & Trust and Settings.

### 36.5 Data integrity release gates

- Every displayed generated value has provenance and actual source/type status.
- Every derived metric has a definition/formula/version and required-input test.
- No placeholder or demo value can silently enter a real record.
- No output is labelled live/observed/forecast/satellite without matching source evidence.
- Repeated identical inputs and versions give reproducible engine results.
- Existing reports do not change because the user edited current inputs later.
- Schedules cannot run on data the backend has no permission to use.
- Every visible interactive control performs a tested action or is explicitly disabled with an explanation.
- Every map boundary edit is validated and versioned.
- Cross-page farm/field/crop navigation opens the exact target entity.

