## 45. Full Advanced Mode science and telemetry content catalogue

This section captures the additional detailed measurement requirements contained in the source material. Treat every item as a requested capability, but only render it as a real measurement after its source/model is implemented and validated. If the source is unavailable, show its explicit status and impact. Do not approximate a missing metric by inventing a plausible value.

### 45.1 Weather and climate data

**Primary planned source:** NASA POWER Daily API, subject to current documentation and variable availability checks. Candidate parameters include daily minimum, maximum and mean temperature; precipitation; solar radiation; wind; relative humidity; and reference evapotranspiration where a suitable series is available. Verify parameter IDs, units, temporal community, climatology vs daily history, and spatial source resolution against the official API before writing connector code.

**Required calculations and displays if supported:**

- Daily minimum, maximum and mean temperature series.
- Daily/weekly/monthly/seasonal precipitation totals with defined periods.
- Solar radiation/insolation series and unit.
- Wind speed and direction, humidity and vapour pressure deficit where required inputs exist.
- Reference evapotranspiration ET₀ using a verified method and required inputs; crop evapotranspiration ETc using sourced crop coefficients and growth stages.
- Growing Degree Days (GDD) accumulated over a declared period, with crop-specific base and upper temperature assumptions. Show the threshold, calculation interval and any caps/floors.
- Heat-stress days only when temperature thresholds are crop-specific and the relevant sensitive growth stage/date is known.
- Seasonal dry-spell duration and rainfall anomaly against a comparable historical reference period.
- A climate trend (e.g. robust trend estimator) only if it is computed from a sufficient historical series for that location and passes the selected trend test. “No clear trend” is a valid output.
- Historical temperature and precipitation variability; dry, typical and wet scenario years chosen by documented quantiles/criteria rather than arbitrary fixed years.
- Weather-normality display: “warmer/drier/wetter than normal” based on a documented local/seasonal baseline and a defined anomaly magnitude; show the reference interval and baseline years in Advanced Mode.

**Important distinctions:** NASA POWER history/analysis data is not automatically a current observation from the exact farm and is not a forward forecast. A dedicated forecast provider is needed for multi-day forecasts. Historical reanalysis is not identical to a weather-station measurement. Show dataset/model type, location/grid, observation period, retrieval time and latency. Never call a climatological monthly average a 30-year daily record. Never add unexplained correction multipliers to GDD/rainfall/ET values. If using daily data, compute from daily values; if using a monthly climatology endpoint, label the output as climatology and do not pretend daily extremes were captured.

### 45.2 Water and evapotranspiration intelligence

The Advanced Water Intelligence view must expose, where available and valid:

- Reference ET₀ and method/source.
- Crop coefficient Kc by growth stage and source.
- Crop ETc / estimated crop demand in mm per day and over the selected period.
- Effective precipitation in mm separately from raw precipitation.
- Farmer-reported irrigation events/amounts and any seasonal irrigation cap/allowance.
- Root-zone water-balance state and its starting assumptions.
- Plant-available water (PAW) only when soil-water retention/available water capacity, rooting depth and model prerequisites exist.
- Readily available water (RAW) and management allowable depletion (MAD) only when crop/soil parameters support the computation.
- Net balance over the selected period; unmet demand and expected stress intervals only when the water-balance model is valid.
- Topsoil versus root-zone moisture, always labelled by product/model/depth; never treat these as interchangeable.
- Daily chronosequence table for the supported period: date; ET₀; Kc/stage; ETc; observed or analysed rain; effective rain; recorded irrigation; net modelled balance; root-zone state if available; and per-value source/status.
- 14-day or other declared demand-versus-supply comparison chart, with the exact period stated.
- Soil water capacity layered diagram/3D column only as a visual explanation of the model, not a sensor display; label surface/root-zone/deep layer and distinguish actual measurements from simulated/estimated states.
- Irrigation guidance only when the water model and relevant local crop/soil information support it; show the assumptions and recommend consulting local agronomic advice where the method is uncertain.
- Water demand, effective rainfall and irrigation must be compared on compatible units and periods; do not claim that a deficit precisely predicts plant stress without crop/stage and soil context.

A calculation explorer should show the exact formula currently implemented, values substituted, unit conversions, Kc stage, weather inputs, assumptions and output. If the FAO-56 Penman–Monteith method is not implemented with required inputs, label the method honestly as an approximation; never use the FAO-56 name for a temperature-only heuristic.

### 45.3 Soil and pedology intelligence

Provide a structured table rather than an arbitrary pile of metrics. Columns: property; value; unit; depth interval; evidence type; source/product; date/retrieval; spatial resolution; uncertainty/range if supplied; confidence; “What it means”; and status.

Potential soil properties where available: sand, silt, clay fractions and textural class; pH; soil organic carbon/organic matter with the conversion assumptions stated; bulk density; coarse fragments; cation exchange capacity; total nitrogen where available; available water capacity where supplied by an appropriate dataset/model; salinity/electrical conductivity only from a source or measurement that actually provides it; drainage classification only from a legitimate source or explicitly identified inference; slope/elevation from suitable terrain data; laboratory test results with lab/date/method/sample depth; and modelled moisture estimates with depth/time basis.

Soil texture composition chart must display sand/silt/clay fractions that sum consistently under documented rounding rules. Soil texture class must be calculated using a stated classification system (e.g., USDA texture triangle) and must not mix classification systems without labels. pH comparison ranges must be crop-specific where relevant; do not imply a single ideal pH range for all crops. If a mapped pH estimate conflicts with a newer lab result, show both and explain the precedence rule; do not overwrite the lab result.

Soil moisture trends can compare topsoil/root zone only if time/depth/variable semantics are compatible. Do not fill a missing time series by interpolating a month of invented data. A soil profile/3D visual must clearly show which layers have measured, mapped, modelled or unavailable properties; do not render a detailed soil profile as though the exact field layers have been sampled when they have not.

### 45.4 Satellite vegetation and surface conditions

Potential products: NASA MODIS vegetation index products (NDVI/EVI), MODIS ET where applicable, or Sentinel-2/Harmonized Landsat Sentinel products if actually connected. Before using them, verify product version, pixel resolution, composite period, cloud/quality flags, revisit/availability, licensing and processing details.

Required outputs when available:

- Index name (NDVI/EVI or other actually calculated index), value/scale, source/product/band, scene or composite date, pixel size and QC/cloud status.
- Field-level aggregate method (e.g. polygon-weighted mean/median, valid-pixel count/coverage) and percentage of the polygon represented by valid pixels.
- Trend over recent weeks/season with baseline method and sample count.
- Stage-aware baseline comparisons only where crop type, stage and historical/reference data support them.
- A clear map layer/legend; separate the raster/index date from the current date.
- A summary such as “vegetation index is lower than its recent baseline” rather than “disease detected” unless the diagnostic method is independently validated.
- A dedicated raw-data/source card or evidence view may be added in Advanced Mode, but do not create a “satellite console” dashboard card simply to display raw telemetry. The source data belongs in organised technical details.

Never create NDVI from precipitation or assign generic NDVI values by rainfall bands. Never apply a fixed global trend number to each farm. If imagery is missing, cloud-obscured, stale or at a resolution that poorly represents the field, show the appropriate status and explain the limitation.

### 45.5 NASA SMAP soil moisture

Use a real SMAP connector only if the selected product endpoint, access method and data use are confirmed. SMAP Level-4 root-zone soil moisture is model-assisted data assimilating radiometer observations into a land-surface model; it is not a direct measurement taken from the exact field. The cited product describes approximately 9 km, 3-hourly estimates with latency, and the relevant product/version must be rechecked before implementation.

For every SMAP value show product/version, surface vs root-zone depth, time interval, timestamp/latency, source grid and aggregation method. If the application uses NASA POWER's `GWETROOT` or any other modelled moisture variable instead of SMAP, the source tag must say that exact product and its modelled type—not “NASA SMAP observed.” A planned connector cannot show a live/connected badge.

### 45.6 GPM IMERG rainfall cross-check

Use suitable NASA GPM IMERG products to cross-check available precipitation datasets and calculate daily/weekly/seasonal totals, respecting each product's accumulation period, latency, units and spatial resolution. Record disagreements between datasets; do not blindly average them. Choose a documented primary source for each calculation and display material discrepancies/quality flags. Near-real-time and retrospectively processed products may differ; don't rewrite a past report without retaining the product and retrieval time used at the time.

### 45.7 Terrain and NASADEM

Potential metrics: elevation, slope and relative within-field terrain where pixel resolution supports them. Derived slope requires a documented calculation and vertical/horizontal unit handling. Terrain can help contextualise possible runoff/erosion susceptibility but does not alone prove waterlogging or soil loss. On small fields, disclose scale mismatch and avoid asserting detailed within-field variation from a coarse data source.

### 45.8 SoilGrids or alternative soil properties

SoilGrids provides mapped predictions for global soil properties at roughly 250 m and standard depth intervals and can include quantified uncertainty. It is a mapped estimate, not a live field measurement. Verify current endpoint/service status before committing to a connector; if a particular endpoint is unavailable, use a verified documented access route/appropriate alternative and clearly label it. Do not derive actual salinity, measured nutrient deficiency, drainage failure or field-specific water-holding capacity from unrelated properties alone.

### 45.9 Dry / typical / wet growing-season stress testing

For each candidate rotation, compare dry, typical and wet historical seasons using documented quantiles/year selection and consistent crop water/rotation model logic. Display selected years/sample size, crop water demand, available rain/irrigation, deficits/stress indicators and uncertainty. Historical scenarios are not forecasts. If a reliable annual series is not available or too short, mark this section limited/unavailable instead of synthesising a fake historical distribution.

## 46. Full crop suitability, AI explanation and professional wording requirements

### 46.1 Crop Suitability is distinct from Crop Rotation Plans

Navigation and page headings must separate:

- **Crop Rotation Plans:** compare multi-season sequences such as Wheat → Chickpea → Sorghum → Cover Crop.
- **Crop Suitability:** compare individual candidate crops for the selected farm/field.

Do not put individual crop compatibility cards under a heading that implies complete rotation plans without a clearly visible sub-navigation. Make the second-level navigation bar visually distinct from its content: sufficient spacing, clear border/background/active underline, readable active label and a separate toolbar for Sort and Filter. The tab labels and sorting controls must never be visually swallowed by paragraphs or overlapping with page headings.

### 46.2 Plain-language percentage explanation

A crop factor percentage means a degree of match under the chosen model, not a directly measured biological percentage. At the top of Crop Suitability, show a brief explanation such as: “Each percentage shows how closely this crop matches one part of your field conditions. A low score points to a mismatch, not the percentage of the crop that will grow.” Add a short glossary for Temperature & Season, Water Match, Soil pH Match and Drainage Match.

For each crop: crop name/photo; overall `XX% Match`; clear subtitle “Overall fit for your farm and preferences”; 3–7 supported factor match bars/values; “Main issue” with a plain-language description; evidence confidence; and action/link to view details or compare. Example labels: “Temperature & growing season — low match; conditions may be too cold or the usable growing period too short.” Do not label factors as “Temp 11%” without explaining what that means. A high pH match means the entered/estimated soil pH is close to the crop's supported preferred range under the model—not that the crop absorbs nutrients with a quantified efficiency unless that is actually modelled.

### 46.3 Crop family, disease and crop placement explanations

Every crop may display its family in plain wording (Grain, Legume, Oilseed, Brassica, etc.) and technical botanical family in Advanced Mode. Explain the role only where supported (e.g., legume nitrogen contribution requires an appropriate model/assumptions; disease break needs crop-host/pathogen knowledge). Explain why the next crop follows the previous crop and what carries forward: nitrogen credit, residue, soil-water state, root structure, family return interval or risk break—only factors present in the actual calculation record.

### 46.4 AI Agronomist / explanation panel

If the AI Agronomist remains in product scope, it must be a side drawer or panel that is context-aware: it knows the selected farm, field, active rotation/report/scenario and relevant source snapshot. It can answer with simple or advanced phrasing based on current mode; show links to source evidence; and be explicit when it does not have enough data. Its response must use a validated structured payload of calculation outputs and source references, not independently estimate or override a score.

Quick prompts should be relevant to the open context, such as “Why was this rotation ranked first?”, “What is the biggest risk for this field?”, “Compare this plan with the alternative”, and “What does this metric mean?” Do not display unsupported AI-generated actions as if they were part of the verified action plan. Every actionable suggestion must pass the same rules as other recommendations.

