# PART III — COMPONENT-LEVEL MATERIAL, TYPOGRAPHY AND ENGINEERING CONTRACTS FOR AUXILIARY SCREENS

This part extends the same specificity to shared and secondary screens. Do not interpret a screen's omission from the main page list as permission to leave it generic. Each component below must use the common design tokens from Section 0A, the shared state patterns from Section 27, and the exact domain records from Sections 31–33.

## 46. Global navigation, top bar, profile menu and command search

### 46.1 Top navigation geometry

Desktop top navigation is a 64px-high full-width row, with a 1px bottom divider `rgba(225,241,245,.10)`, background `rgba(5,18,25,.88)` (88% opaque) and `backdrop-filter: blur(16px) saturate(115%)` only where the surface genuinely overlays imagery. Use a centered max-width layout aligned with the page container, 24–32px horizontal padding, and a three-zone flex layout: brand on the left; primary route navigation in the middle; utility controls on the right. The wordmark uses 14–16px semibold type with a small leaf/satellite-field glyph; do not use oversized logo treatment. Navigation labels are 13–14px, 44px minimum hit area, with active state indicated by text color, a 2px bottom indicator and semantic `aria-current=page`. Do not use a permanent glow.

Utility actions: Undo, Redo, global search, help, notifications and profile/settings. Buttons are 36–40px visual size with 44px hit areas; tooltips appear after a modest delay for mouse and are accessible by focus/tap. Disabled Undo/Redo includes a reason in the tooltip and accessible description. The bar must not wrap unpredictably; at 1024px condense navigation into a menu; at 768px use an explicit mobile navigation pattern and preserve selected farm context.

### 46.2 Farm/field selector

The selector shows the active farm label, optional field label and chevron. Dropdown width 300–380px desktop, max viewport width minus 24px, max height 70vh with internal scroll, search box and hierarchical farms → fields grouping. Each row contains name, location or area context, and a status label where valid. Selected row has background `rgba(39,199,178,.10)`, 2px or inset accent marker and check icon. Selecting a farm triggers one global selection command, closes the selector, updates all dependent views and aborts/ignores stale in-flight queries. If unsaved edits belong to a different field, show Save / Discard / Stay; do not silently discard.

### 46.3 Command search

The command launcher is a centered modal (desktop width 560–680px; mobile full-screen sheet) with 96% opaque surface, 16–20px radius, 24px padding and shadow token. Search input sits at the top; group results by Farms, Fields, Rotation plans, Reports, Alerts, Tasks and Data sources. Each result shows entity type, primary name, parent path and relevant date/status. Keyboard behavior: Ctrl/Cmd+K opens if shortcut is advertised; arrows move selection; Enter opens; Escape closes; focus returns to invoker. Query only accessible records. A result must carry stable entity IDs and route to exact context; matching only by crop/farm name is not sufficient. Show debounced search/pending, no-results, unavailable/error states.

### 46.4 Profile menu and session states

Profile menu includes settings, data export, account/session state and sign-in/out only where real auth is implemented. Do not display an avatar/username as proof of authenticated account if it is only a local profile preference. If auth is not integrated, label local workspace state honestly. Menu rows have icon, label, optional helper and 44px target. Authentication errors are typed and never simulated as success.

## 47. AI Agronomist chat and AI-generated explanations

### 47.1 Chat drawer visual specification

Desktop opens as a 420–480px right side panel from the app shell, height `calc(100dvh - 64px)` below the header, with a 1px left border and surface `--fs-modal-surface` (96% opaque). Header displays `Field Shift Agronomist`, a concise selected farm/field context chip, source/caveat indicator and close control. Conversation body scrolls independently. User messages align right with a muted teal-tinted fill (`rgba(39,199,178,.10)`) and assistant messages align left on a neutral surface; never use exaggerated bubble radii, purple gradients or “AI sparkle” decoration in every response. Body 14–15px with 1.45–1.6 line-height. The composer is sticky at the bottom with multiline input, send button, disabled/sending state and a brief privacy/context disclosure.

### 47.2 Context and response constraints

A chat request passes only the minimal selected context needed: farm/field IDs, approved profile fields, selected rotation/report and cited evidence identifiers. Avoid silently sending the whole farm archive or all journal text. The assistant may explain known calculations and help navigate; it must not invent current telemetry, new calculations or external source status. A generated claim referencing a result must include a link to the underlying Evidence Drawer/report. If no data is available, state that and offer a route to add it. Distinguish deterministic model output from generated natural-language explanation.

Backend endpoint contract uses a typed request (`message`, bounded history, context reference IDs, locale, request ID) and structured response (`reply`, evidenceRefs, warnings, model/version, createdAt). Validate size/rate limits; sanitize/render Markdown safely; never execute returned markup. On quota/network refusal show a retryable explanatory state while keeping draft text. Never claim a request succeeded before the service response. Do not persist a chat by default unless policy/consent explicitly says so; let the user clear conversation history if it is persisted.

## 48. Crop suitability detail / crop catalogue / crop picker

### 48.1 Crop browser and suitability layout

Crop catalogue is a searchable/filterable table or gallery with real, accurate crop photographs only when available. Desktop filter toolbar: crop name search, family, season/growing window and available-for-this-field filter. Use a wide table for technical comparisons and a photo tile gallery for exploratory selection; do not merge both into a wall of cards. Crop image cards use 4:3 thumbnail, object-fit cover, 8px radius and a stable 16px name label below. Their visual states include eligible, selected, low-confidence, unsuitable and unavailable, each with non-color labels/icons.

Suitability detail opens a full-width page or 440–520px drawer depending on entry point. It shows crop name/family, score/rating and confidence as separate concepts, primary suitability drivers, limiting factors, local timing window only if supported, water/soil fit, source coverage and the next action. In Advanced Mode, expose factor breakdown values, input metrics/units, thresholds, weights and crop knowledge-base provenance. `Add to candidate comparison` updates the active comparison set; it does not mutate the selected live rotation. `Use in new plan` creates a new draft with provenance intact.

### 48.2 Calculation semantics

Crop suitability is not rotation suitability. Evaluate crop-specific temperature, GDD, frost/heat exposure, water, soil texture/pH/salinity/drainage, slope and growing-window constraints only if the knowledge base contains the required thresholds and applicable data. A weighted geometric mean or other declared aggregation method must be implemented exactly as specified in the model registry; a critical limiting factor may cap the score through a documented rule. Missing inputs reduce evidence confidence or cause `insufficient evidence`; they do not silently receive an ideal value. UI displays the main limiting factor and what information could resolve it. A crop cannot be shown as “unsuitable” merely because a data connector failed.

## 49. Field Journal, observation capture and attachments

The journal is a field-context timeline and entry form, not a free-floating chat screen. The page uses a 7-column timeline/list and 5-column detail/editor layout desktop; mobile stacks the list and entry editor. Journal entries have date/time, farm/field, event category, narrative, optional structured metric/unit, optional photo, `observed` vs `planned` vs `estimated` basis and author/source. Use 14–15px body, 12–13px metadata and an 86% opaque editor panel for readable fields over an image backdrop. Photo thumbnails preserve aspect ratio and open into an accessible lightbox with captions; don't add decorative stock photos to replace user evidence.

Saving creates an immutable observation entry or a new version if explicitly editing a prior entry. Keep original text when AI parses it into structure. Parsed quantities must carry a confidence/ambiguity flag and require confirmation before affecting calculations. The entry date, crop stage and field ID are validated; an attachment upload has max-size/type constraints and a retry state. A journal event may inform later analysis but must not retroactively alter a prior report or automatically assert unmentioned facts (e.g. a note about a faulty pump does not prove irrigation was absent for a precise duration).

## 50. Exportable Field Brief / print / PDF contract

Field Brief has a preview page designed like a professional agronomic briefing, not the dark dashboard screen printed directly. Its first page has a restrained agricultural cover image across 20–28% of the page height, dark readable title block, farm/field scope, report date, version and evidence status. Below, show ranked findings, top recommended actions, key crop plan and a compact data confidence/source note. Further pages contain field-specific evidence, sources and methodology. Use consistent A4/Letter-responsive print CSS, margins ~15–18mm, 10–11pt body for printable output, proper page breaks and table header repetition. Preserve units and caveats. Never print hidden UI buttons, navigation, loading indicators, unsupported metrics or demo data without a visible demo label.

Export must use a frozen `reportId`/snapshot, not make fresh calculations during download. PDF export UI has `Preparing`, `Ready`, `Failed`, and retry states. If PDF generation is not implemented, do not render a functional-looking `Download PDF` button; either implement a tested print-to-PDF route or expose an honest alternative. Files are named using safe identifiers/date (e.g. `field-shift-field-report-YYYY-MM-DD.pdf`) and must not include private coordinates in the filename.

## 51. In-app Help / page guide / glossary

The `?` button opens a contextual accessible dialog or side panel with 92–96% opaque surface, 16px radius, 24px padding and focus trap. Contents: page purpose; explanation of the current component/metric; 2–3 appropriate next steps; definitions of technical terms; link to source/methodology. The help content is keyed by `route + mode + componentKey` so it is not generic. Closing returns focus to the invoking control. Help text is written in plain language; the underlying technical glossary can state full developer/agronomic terminology. Do not place a huge tutorial card permanently on Home after setup.

## 52. Shared empty, loading, error, stale and partial-data visuals

These states are reusable design-system primitives and must be consistent across every page:

- **Loading:** content-shaped skeletons that match the final component geometry; no blank page, no unlabelled spinner and no fake progress. Skeleton uses neutral surface tokens and subtle static shimmer only when reduced motion permits.
- **Empty:** a meaningful state icon (20–24px line icon), 16–18px title, one sentence explaining why there is no content, and one real next action. No fake metrics or placeholder graphs.
- **Partial:** small amber/neutral status label plus a clear list of missing inputs/source outcomes and what part of the result remains supported.
- **Stale:** visible `Out of date` label with changed dependency reason, last computed time and `Refresh / rerun` action; the old report remains accessible and is explicitly historical.
- **Unavailable:** value text says `Unavailable` with reason; never show `0`, `N/A` or dash without distinction if that could be interpreted as an actual zero.
- **Error:** plain-language cause, retryability and action; technical error/correlation ID available in Advanced Mode, not raw stack trace.
- **Saving:** button disabled only for the operation being submitted, inline `Saving…` text, then success or failure. Avoid page-wide modal blocking for a simple local save.
- **Conflict:** if version changed in another context, show `This record changed since you opened it`, display timestamp/version and offer reload or compare/merge.

Every primitive must specify visual styling through tokens, `aria-live`/role only as appropriate, keyboard focus order and a test for state transition. The state UI is never an optional polish step.

## 53. Motion curves and performance budgets by component class

Use the following defaults and tune only through documented visual QA:

| Motion category | Default duration | Easing | Allowed use |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Button/field micro-interaction | 120–180ms | `ease-out` | pressed, hover, focus feedback |
| Tooltip/popover | 140–180ms | `ease-out` | opacity + 2–4px translation |
| Drawer/modal | 200–280ms | cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1) | translate + opacity; preserve focus |
| Page transition | 220–320ms | `ease-in-out` | 8–12px y movement + opacity, not full-screen zoom |
| Wizard question change | 200–280ms | `ease-out` | horizontal/fade; reverse on Back |
| Chart reveal | 400–700ms once | `ease-out` | draw/reveal actual series, only after data loads |
| Atmospheric photo drift | 30–90s | linear/ease-in-out | optional, low-motion hero only |

Do not animate large card surfaces on every re-render. Animate only when state transition merits feedback. Don't use blur/filter animations that cause heavy repaint on weak devices. `prefers-reduced-motion` disables ambient drift, parallax, chart drawing and movement; retain immediate state feedback. Respect user control to disable motion. Lazy-load below-the-fold photos, provide `width`/`height` or aspect-ratio to avoid layout shift, use optimized WebP/AVIF where supported, and retain a local/fallback image if remote assets fail. A photo's failure must not hide the content.

## 54. Reference implementation terminology checklist — builder must be precise in code and handoff

The code handoff must use and explain the following terms in the implementation inventory:

- `CSS custom properties/design tokens` for colours, alphas, blur, spacing, border/radius, shadows and motion;
- `CSS Grid/Flexbox` for primary page geometry; `position:absolute` reserved for overlays, map controls, markers and local ornaments only;
- `stacking context/z-index` for hero imagery, scrims, glass surfaces, controls, tooltips and dialogs;
- `backdrop-filter` plus alpha background for glass panels; solid fallback and contrast verification;
- `responsive breakpoints`, `container sizing`, `intrinsic aspect ratio`, `object-fit`, `object-position` for photography;
- `controlled component`, `derived state`, `state machine`, `domain command`, `optimistic mutation with rollback`, `route context` for interactions;
- `DTO`, `schema validation`, `authorization`, `idempotency`, `optimistic concurrency`, `SSE/polling`, `retry/backoff`, `correlation ID`, `cache key`, `invalidation dependency` for backend;
- `immutable snapshot`, `versioned entity`, `data provenance`, `model version`, `metric registry`, `confidence method`, `partial success` for scientific integrity;
- `unit test`, `property test`, `integration test`, `E2E test`, `visual regression`, `WCAG contrast/focus` for verification.

Use jargon to increase implementation precision, not to hide ambiguity. Every technical term in the product UI should be translated for ordinary farmers via glossary/help, while the builder specification can use the precise engineering name.

